pe` first (the row table fixes `:tail` at three slots); amend the refusal row and note that emit silently drops extra slots from an abstract node, so a tamper must be a raw row edit with rehash.\n7\tB4: section 7.1 order: the dependency closure runs after the walk (segments are known only then) and before frame validation and emit; also the lower's staged checks across the whole task tree in the order stores, pendings and install records, dependencies (requires/code agreement, trees, vector loads under its A, closure origin and binders, stores-of census per task, primitives), custody, THEN attachment (zero attaches before every refusal).\n8\tAlso document the other decisions the code made that the design is silent on: per-context dependency discharge (answered by the context's own E, then the one active store, then primitive, installing export, registry) and control-flow reachability from the resume pc (`live-pcs`; note the parser refuses backward jumps so the reachability function is tested directly), `:source-index` as a lift input for tree association, thrown refusals, custody always nil at the root (exclusive/holder is phase 7), linked registry entries not travelling, the whole store travelling, the lower's own refusal kinds (`:role :custody :incomplete-install :kind :parked-id :requires :segment :install-phase :install-response :install-child :closure-origin :closure-binders` and the `:yin.k/stores` missing census); and list as OPEN QUESTIONS (not decided by you) anything that looks like a design choice the owner should see. Also record the `handoff.cljc` finding as a note in the doc or the report: `export-task` builds installing names with `(symbol (str m \"/\" x))`, a nil-namespace symbol that never equals `m/x`; do not edit code.\n9\tForeground only. Report: 15 lines plain summary first, then the list of edits by section, then open questions; ends READY or NOT READY for codex's doc review.\n10\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"02df6bdc-04b9-48af-