{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012HTWB35TEHB3Qj1qQggXGQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation: phase 6, the continuation format and the row carrier\n2\t\n3\tStatus: **Companion design, phase 6 of `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`\n4\t§10.** Written 2026-10-11 (Architect: claude-fable-5-1) from the frozen\n5\tdesign as amended (`8f0b9e38`), the landed 4a evaluator\n6\t(`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc`) and the 4b stage-1 evaluator\n7\ton branch `srvm-phase4b` (read only), the merged phase-5 derivations\n8\tdesign, the UCF document and its version-2 amendment, the row-carrier\n9\tdraft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`, body version 3, never landed),\n10\tthe two de Bruijn kernels, and the landed UCF code and harness\n11\t(`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc`,\n12\t`test/yin/vm/ucf/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`). Revision 2 (2026-10-11)\n13\tapplies the codex review\n14\t`collab/1791760000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n15\t(CHANGES_REQUESTED): the task's free environment is carried explicitly\n16\t(§4.4, §6.3), the stack kernel's K slices and tail completion are\n17\tcorrected and gated (§4.3, §8.3), register return destinations are\n18\trecovered from A (§4.2), `act` and `tail` have separate algorithms, the\n19\tvalidator has a complete pure input contract and the carrier's order\n20\t(§3), the static laws use actual-successor live-out (§5.6), blinding is\n21\ta specified re-emission (§5.5), the harness matrix is expanded (§5.2),\n22\t6a is the independent codec (§8.1), and the amendment inventory is\n23\texhaustive (§10). Revision 3 (2026-10-11) applies the codex round-2\n24\treview\n25\t`collab/1791800000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n26\t(CHANGES_REQUESTED): stack lowering bases are cumulative through each\n27\tslice (§4.3), the live operand subtracts the virtual destination before\n28\tmapping (§4.2, §5.6), the free environment's source on lift is specified\n29\twith its owner (§6.3), E2 requires lexical presence and X1 validates the\n30\troot's free environment (§3.2), the harness oracles of rows 15, 20, 22\n31\tand 23 and the segment-wrapper results are corrected (§5.2), the\n32\tcross-lane re-emission takes an explicit stream correspondence (§5.5),\n33\tand the amendment residue is closed (§10.3 R9, U7). The frozen document\n34\tis not changed by this one.\n35\tSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md), including its\n36\tunitarity invariant, and to\n37\t[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md)\n38\t(\"the frozen design\"); format and rigor model:\n39\t[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md)\n40\tand [`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md).\n41\t\n42\tEvery section marks its content **DECIDED** (an owner ruling, frozen\n43\ttext, or code that exists) or **PROPOSED** (a design choice of this\n44\tdocument, open to the reviewer). File references are to the worktree at\n45\t`8f0b9e38`; line numbers are those of that commit; the 4b branch is\n46\tcited as `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc`, with the line numbers of\n47\tits stage-1 commit (`0f4522b9`) unless a citation says stage 2\n48\t(`bdf48cf4`, committed after revision 2; stage 2 moved the reified\n49\tpayload to `:256`, `create-vm` to `:349-375` and `gc-roots`/`gc-children`\n50\tto `:485-488`, and changed none of the shapes this document reads).\n51\t\n52\tContents: §0 rulings and the one-paragraph answer · §1 the canonical\n53\tframe · §2 the row carrier · §3 the frame validator · §4 layouts · §5\n54\tthe safepoint harness · §6 code and task context in the carrier · §7\n55\tlift and lower · §8 slices, gates and phase 7's entry conditions · §9\n56\tverification honesty · §10 findings and amendments · §11 owner\n57\tquestions · §12 portability.\n58\t\n59\t---\n60\t\n61\t## 0. Rulings and the one-paragraph answer\n62\t\n63\t### 0.1 Rulings that bind this design (DECIDED)\n64\t\n65\t1. **A is the only code identity**; new identities digest under Jing's\n66\t   default (BLAKE3), never SHA-256 (`ucf/code-address` is\n67\t   `jing/segment-key`, `ucf.cljc:247-251`).\n68\t2. **No legacy**: one body version, no reader for older bodies, no\n69\t   compat shim, no migration path; clean breaks.\n70\t3. **Unitarity**: the AST is never lost and is always retrievable\n71\t   wherever code is lowered; no design assumes a tree-less receiver; the\n72\t   segment-to-tree association of the phase-5 design (§9.2 there) is a\n73\t   given, including its completeness rule: an absent entry **or an empty\n74\t   tree set** is incomplete.\n75\t4. **The vector-to-datoms mapping is retired**; the carrier carries\n76\t   positional tuples and rows, never datoms-from-vector.\n77\t5. **dao.stream is the complexity boundary**: no transport mechanics.\n78\t6. **One truth, many interpretations**: the register machine's\n79\t   configuration `C = ⟨seg, pc, W⟩` with `E`, `K` is the canonical frame\n80\t   shape; the stack and register kernels reconstruct it through their\n81\t   layouts (frozen §4.3, §10 phase 6).\n82\t7. **No file of phase 4b or phase 5 is touched**, so this phase runs\n83\t   concurrently with them (§8.5 names the dependencies that remain, and\n84\t   the one old-kernel file 6c must change).\n85\t\n86\t### 0.2 The answer\n87\t\n88\tA continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register\n89\tmachine, `act` or `tail`, with its saved window, its `K` of `:return`\n90\tframes, its environment, its delivery record and its pending wait\n91\t(frozen §2.4, §4.2), plus the task context it needs: the task's **free\n92\tenvironment**, store slice, module stores, parked table, cursor cells, code by\n93\tA and the tree association. **§1** restates the frame as the 4b\n94\tevaluator mints it. **§2** gives the row carrier: body version 3, one\n95\tprofile (`\"v4\"`), Merkle content rows under Jing's default digest, the\n96\twindow as a sorted keyed row in which a nil value is a present\n97\t`[:lit nil]`, `:act`, `:tail`, `:window`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and\n98\t`:parked-table` rows replacing the stale stack-shaped rows, a root\n99\t`free-env` slot, the pending rows and census unchanged. **§3** gives the\n100\tvalidator as an ordered set of rules over the decoded frame, the carried\n101\tcode and decoded task context, engine-independent, run after canonical\n102\tnumbering as the carrier orders it. **§4** defines the two layouts with\n103\tseparate `act` and `tail` algorithms: the register kernel's physical\n104\tregisters are paired with A's virtual ones pc by pc and its return\n105\tdestinations recovered from A's call; the stack kernel's operand stack\n106\tis sliced `[previous-base, base_i)` per frame, and its tail completion is\n107\ta kernel seam this phase must add and gate. **§5** is the harness over\n108\tthree lanes with an expanded, concrete matrix and a specified\n109\tre-emission procedure for cross-lane equality. **§6** fixes what travels\n110\tfor code and task context. **§8** cuts the slices: 6a the independent\n111\tframe and row codec, 6b full task transport (needs phase 5's scanners\n112\tand 4b stage 2), 6c the layouts and the stack tail-completion seam\n113\t(needs 5a/5b).\n114\t\n115\t---\n116\t\n117\t## 1. The canonical frame\n118\t\n119\t### 1.1 Shapes, as the evaluator mints them\n120\t\n121\t**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4 \"Delivery\", §4.2; evaluator design §1.3-1.6;\n122\t`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:61-84` for `act-payload`,\n123\t`entry-builders`, `tail-builders`; `:229-234` for the reified payload\n124\tand the park). Two closed state shapes and one closed delivery record:\n125\t\n126\t```clojure\n127\tact  = {:segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :k K :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd r}}\n128\ttail = {:segment seg :site n :k K :deliver {:deliver :return}}\n129\tret  = {:type :return :segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :rd r}     ; one frame kind of K\n130\t```\n131\t\n132\t- `:pc` of an `act` is the resume pc, already `pc+1` of the parking\n133\t  instruction; `:site` of a `tail` is the tail call's own pc (evaluator\n134\t  §1.5).\n135\t- `W` of an `act` is `saved(pc, rd) = W↾(L(pc) − {rd})`\n136\t  (`analysis/saved`, `analysis.cljc:92-97`), built with `select-keys`,\n137\t  so a nil-valued live register is a present key.\n138\t- `K` is a vector of `ret` frames, innermost last, or nil when empty\n139\t  (`put-registers`, `semantic_register.cljc:47-52`); each frame's window\n140\t  is `saved(frame.pc, frame.rd)` of its own body.\n141\t- A **parked record** (`:park`, and FFI bookkeeping under a call id) is\n142\t  an `act` payload plus `:type :parked-continuation :id`\n143\t  (`engine/park-continuation`; evaluator §1.6); the FFI bookkeeping\n144\t  record of an effectful **tail** call is a `tail` payload (evaluator\n145\t  §1.6).\n146\t- A **reified continuation** is `values/continuation owner (assoc act\n147\t  :type :reified-continuation)` (`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:229-232`).\n148\t- The wait entry the engine holds is the `act` or `tail` payload plus\n149\t  the engine's fields (`:reason`, `:cursor-ref`, `:stream-id`, `:datom`,\n150\t  `:call-id`, `:request-sent`, the link fields), which travel as the\n151\t  **pending** (§2.4), never as frame slots.\n152\t- The task's **free environment** is the `:env` a `create-vm` received\n153\t  (`semantic_register.cljc:261`): every activation's `E` is that map\n154\t  merged under the lexical chain's bindings (§4.4), and a de Bruijn\n155\t  kernel holds the same map as its task-wide `:free-env`\n156\t  (`debruijn/stack.cljc:82, 418, 683`; `debruijn/register.cljc:50`).\n157\t\n158\t### 1.2 The UCF frame, keyed form\n159\t\n160\t**DECIDED** (frozen §4.2), restated with one naming decision (§10.1 F1):\n161\t\n162\t```clojure\n163\t;; act state\n164\t{:yin.k/state :act\n165\t :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/pc n\n166\t :yin.k/env   {:yin.k/bindings {sym V …} :yin.k/store-of M?}\n167\t :yin.k/window {r V …}                      ; exactly saved(pc, rd); sorted by r on the wire\n168\t :yin.k/deliver {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}\n169\t :yin.k/k [{:yin.k/frame-type :return :yin.k/segment A :yin.k/pc n\n170\t            :yin.k/env E :yin.k/window {r V …} :yin.k/rd r} …]\n171\t :yin.k/pending {…}}                        ; absent on a parked record and a reified value\n172\t\n173\t;; tail state\n174\t{:yin.k/state :tail\n175\t :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/site n\n176\t :yin.k/deliver {:yin.k/deliver :return}\n177\t :yin.k/k [ … ]\n178\t :yin.k/pending {…}}\n179\t```\n180\t\n181\tThis keyed form is the **decoded canonical frame**: what the validator\n182\tof §3 judges, what the layouts of §4 produce and consume, and what the\n183\tharness compares across lanes. The rows of §2 are its carrier and the\n184\ttwo are in bijection (§2.6).\n185\t\n186\t---\n187\t\n188\t## 2. The row carrier\n189\t\n190\tThe row-carrier draft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`) is adopted\n191\t**except** where it describes the stack-shaped machine or legacy\n192\treaders; §10.3 lists every replaced or deleted clause. Its §3 (rows, id\n193\tspaces, census, the explicit \"version 3 verifies under BLAKE3\n194\tpermanently\" rule), §4 (value union), §5.1 (value rows, except\n195\t`:reified`), §5.3 (pending), §5.4 (task context, except\n196\t`:parked-table`), §6 (canonical cursor-cell numbering), §7 (equality) and §8\n197\tsteps 1-6 and 8-12 stand.\n198\t\n199\t### 2.1 Positioning: body version 3, one profile\n200\t\n201\t**DECIDED** by ruling 2 and ruling 6. The carrier is **body version 3**.\n202\tVersions 0-2 are the old handoff's; the row reader refuses them at the\n203\tgate (`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`) and never reads, normalises or upgrades\n204\tone. Their reader (`yin.vm.ucf.handoff`) is untouched by this phase and\n205\tleaves with the stack-shaped machine at phase 8; that coexistence is\n206\tsequencing, not permission for the row reader to accept a legacy body.\n207\tThe wire unit is the draft's:\n208\t\n209\t```clojure\n210\t{:yin.k/handoff true  :yin.k/version 3  :yin.k/root T  :yin.k/rows [[id kind & slots] …]}\n211\t```\n212\t\n213\t**PROPOSED** (reviewer: ACCEPTED). The profile map is\n214\t`{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}` and nothing else; there is\n215\t**no `:yin.k/engine` key**. The frame is the canonical machine's and\n216\tengine-independent by ruling 6; the kernel that executes it is the\n217\treceiving composition's choice. The version-2 registry\n218\t(`ucf/profiles`, `ucf.cljc:51-74`) is not consulted.\n219\t\n220\t### 2.2 Rows, ids, census\n221\t\n222\t**DECIDED** (draft §3, ruling 1). Every row is `[id kind & slots]`;\n223\tcontent rows have `id = (jing/segment-key body)` under Jing's default\n224\tdigest, BLAKE3 for version 3 permanently; identity rows are cursor cells\n225\t`:yin.k/cc-<n>` numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n226\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`); the root carries\n227\tthe cursor-cell census. (The words cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref and cell\n228\tare used exactly as defined in UCF §7.5.3, \"Terminology: cursor, cursor\n229\tcell, cursor-ref, cell\". Before 2026-10-12 the cursor cells were called\n230\t\"cells\" and spelled `:yin.k/c-<n>`, `:cells`, `:cell-body`; the version-2\n231\thandoff carrier keeps those spellings.) One ordering rule: \"sorted\" is by the Jing canonical\n232\tbytes of the key as spelled.\n233\t\n234\t### 2.3 The frame rows (replacing draft §5.2)\n235\t\n236\t**DECIDED** by frozen §4.2's last paragraph; **PROPOSED** names `:act`\n237\tand `:tail` (§10.1 F1, reviewer: ACCEPTED):\n238\t\n239\t| Kind | Slots | Notes |\n240\t|---|---|---|\n241\t| `:act` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  k-cref  rd-N` | the delivery record is `{:deliver :rd :rd rd-N}` by kind |\n242\t| `:tail` | `segment-A  site-N  k-cref` | delivery `{:deliver :return}` by kind; **no** env, window or pc slot |\n243\t| `:window` | `r₁ V₁  r₂ V₂  …` sorted by `rᵢ` ascending, unique | `rᵢ` is `N`; **a nil-valued register is present as `rᵢ [:lit nil]`**; the empty window is `[:window]` |\n244\t| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` outermost first | `[:k]` is the empty continuation |\n245\t| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  rd-N` | one `:return` frame |\n246\t| `:wait` | `state-cref  pending-cref` | `state-cref` is an `:act` or a `:tail` row |\n247\t| `:reified` | `act-cref` | a captured continuation value; its act's `pc−1` is a `:current-continuation` |\n248\t| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | unchanged |\n249\t| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | unchanged (draft §5.1) |\n250\t| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁  S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | unchanged |\n251\t\n252\t`:window`, `:bindings`, `:store` and every other tabled keyed row are\n253\tstructural rows, never a `V`; the draft's \"a reference-free collection\n254\tmust be `[:lit d]`\" rule is about guest collections. `rd` on `:act` is\n255\tredundant with the instruction at `pc−1` and is checked (rule A2), as\n256\t`pc` is redundant with the pending and checked.\n257\t\n258\t### 2.4 Pending rows\n259\t\n260\t**DECIDED** (draft §5.3, UCF §7.4.3, v2 amendment §2): one kind\n261\t`:pending`; reasons `:next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request\n262\t:link-response :install` with the draft's slots, unchanged. What\n263\tchanges is only which state a pending rides on: a `:wait` pairs a\n264\tpending with an `:act` (write-result) **or a `:tail`** (through-return),\n265\tthe frozen §4.1 table's two state columns. The 4b evaluator's in-VM\n266\tmarkers (`:request-sent`, `:call-id`, `:op`, `:datom`,\n267\t`:response-cursor`, `:response-stream`) are the pending's\n268\t(`handoff.cljc:702-724`, `:3219-3229`).\n269\t\n270\t### 2.5 Captures, parked records, module contexts, the parked table\n271\t\n272\t**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4; evaluator §1.6; draft §5.1, §5.4).\n273\t\n274\t- A **reified continuation** value is `[:row B]`, `B` a `:reified\n275\t  [act-cref]` row; the act has no pending, its `pc−1` is a\n276\t  `:current-continuation`, `rd` is that instruction's destination, the\n277\t  window is `saved(pc, rd)`.\n278\t- The **parked table** is `[:parked-table P₁ state-cref₁ P₂ state-cref₂ …]`\n279\t  sorted by `P`; a state is an `:act` whose `pc−1` is `:park` (an\n280\t  explicit park), **or** FFI bookkeeping under a call id `P` that a live\n281\t  `:ffi`/`:ffi-request` pending of the same task carries: an `:act` whose\n282\t  `pc−1` is `:ffi-call`, or a `:tail` whose site is the parking tail\n283\t  `:call`. An active `parked-id` never selects a bookkeeping record (v2\n284\t  amendment §7.3, kept).\n285\t- A **module context** is `E`'s `store-of`; the module store travels as\n286\t  `[:module-store M store-cref]`; every `store-of` resolves to a carried\n287\t  module store (draft §8 step 3).\n288\t- **Install children** are whole `:task` rows in the child role (draft\n289\t  §5.4, §5.5), unchanged.\n290\t\n291\t### 2.6 The root (replacing draft §5.5 in two slots)\n292\t\n293\t**PROPOSED**, with the reviewer's correction of the free environment:\n294\t\n295\t```clojure\n296\t[T :task\n297\t   version          ; 3\n298\t   contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}\n299\t   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n300\t   role             ; :root | :child\n301\t   id-counter       ; N\n302\t   free-env-cref    ; NEW: a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (§6.3)\n303\t   store-cref\n304\t   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n305\t   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n306\t   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n307\t   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (§6.1)\n308\t   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n309\t   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n310\t   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, in wait order     (:blocked, :parked)\n311\t   parked-id        ; P or nil                                (:parked only)\n312\t   result           ; V or nil                                (:halted only)\n313\t   custody]         ; plain, draft §5.5's table\n314\t```\n315\t\n316\tNo `layout` slot and no native image: those were the positional\n317\tprofiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1). The custody table of the draft §5.5\n318\tstands, with its version-0 comparison reworded (§10.3 R10).\n319\t\n320\t### 2.7 The bijection with the keyed frame\n321\t\n322\t**PROPOSED.** `rows->frame` and `frame->rows` (`yin.vm.ucf.rows`, §8.1)\n323\tare inverse on valid row sets and canonical keyed frames: `:act` ↔\n324\t`{:yin.k/state :act …}` with the deliver record synthesised from the\n325\tkind and `rd`; `:tail` ↔ `{:yin.k/state :tail …}`; `:window` ↔\n326\t`:yin.k/window` as a map (`[:lit nil]` ↔ a nil-valued key); `:k` ↔\n327\t`:yin.k/k` in order. `rows->frame` reshapes and decides nothing the\n328\tvalidator decides.\n329\t\n330\t---\n331\t\n332\t## 3. The frame validator\n333\t\n334\t### 3.1 The pure input contract\n335\t\n336\t**DECIDED** (frozen §4.2: membership recomputed from A; representation,\n337\tbody scope, site/pc pairing, destination equality, delivery mode,\n338\treturn-frame sites, `K` structure, pending compatibility). **PROPOSED**\n339\t(reviewer: publish the complete contract):\n340\t\n341\t```clojure\n342\t(frame/defect frame code context task)\n343\t;; frame   : a decoded canonical frame (§1.2), any shape (the rules judge it)\n344\t;; code    : {A vector}, every vector already code/well-formed? (step 4 of the carrier ran)\n345\t;; context : :wait | :captured | :parked\n346\t;; task    : decoded task context, plain data, no engine:\n347\t;;           {:parked-key P-or-nil                 ; the parked-table key this frame sits under (:parked only)\n348\t;;            :ffi-call-ids #{…}                   ; call ids of the task's live :ffi and :ffi-request pendings\n349\t;;            :free-env {sym V …}}                 ; the root's free environment, decoded\n350\t;; => nil, or {:yin.k/kind k :yin.k/path p …}\n351\t\n352\t(frame/task-defect task)\n353\t;; the once-per-body rule X1 over the decoded task context (its :free-env);\n354\t;; run before any frame of the body; => nil or {:yin.k/kind k :yin.k/path p …}\n355\t```\n356\t\n357\tNamespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.frame`, file\n358\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc`, requiring only\n359\t`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `yin.vm.debruijn`\n360\t(`resolve-name`, for the chain of rule E2) and `yin.vm`\n361\t(`reserved-name?`). It knows no kernel and reads no engine: `task` is\n362\tdecoded from the rows (the parked table's key, the pendings' call ids,\n363\tthe root's `free-env`), never from a running machine. A frame a stack\n364\tkernel lifted and one the register evaluator lifted are judged by the\n365\tsame function on the same inputs.\n366\t\n367\t### 3.2 The rules\n368\t\n369\tRules run in the order listed; the first defect wins; every defect\n370\tcarries `:yin.k/kind` and `:yin.k/path`. **Structural rules S1-S4 run\n371\tbefore any dependent read**, so later rules may index freely. `a =\n372\t(analysis/analyze v)` per segment; `body(p) = (code/body-index\n373\t(code/body-ranges v) p)`; `k(body)` the body's register count\n374\t(`code/parse`); `inst(p) = (nth v p)`; `chain(b)` the owner chain of\n375\tbody `b` as phase-5 §1.2 (closure pcs innermost first) and `params(c) =\n376\t(nth (nth v c) 2)`.\n377\t\n378\t**Structure (every frame).**\n379\t\n380\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n381\t|---|---|---|\n382\t| S1 | `frame` is a map; `:yin.k/state` is `:act` or `:tail`; an `act` has exactly the keys `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k` plus optionally `:yin.k/pending`; a `tail` exactly `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/site :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k` plus optionally `:yin.k/pending` | `:frame-shape` |\n383\t| S2 | `:yin.k/k` is a vector; every element is a map with exactly `:yin.k/frame-type :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/rd`, `:yin.k/frame-type` is `:return` | `:k-shape` (path `[:yin.k/k i]`) |\n384\t| S3 | every `:yin.k/window` present (the act's, each return frame's) is a map whose keys are exact non-negative integers (on the wire: sorted ascending, unique) and whose values are `V` | `:window-representation` |\n385\t| S4 | every `:yin.k/env` present is `{:yin.k/bindings {S V …}}` optionally with `:yin.k/store-of M`; `:yin.k/deliver` is a map | `:env-shape` / `:deliver-shape` |\n386\t\n387\t**Code (every frame).**\n388\t\n389\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n390\t|---|---|---|\n391\t| C1 | `:yin.k/segment` of the frame and of every return frame names a key of `code` | `:segment` |\n392\t| C2 | an `act`'s `pc`, every return frame's `pc` and a `tail`'s `site` are **exact non-negative integers** (`cbor/numeric-kind` `:integer` for a wire value; never a float carrier), checked **before** any indexing; then `0 < pc < (count v)` and `0 ≤ site < (count v)` | `:pc-kind` / `:pc-bounds` |\n393\t| C3 | `body(pc−1) = body(pc)` for the act and every return frame (no body ends in a parking instruction, frozen §3.4 rule 6) | `:pc-body` |\n394\t\n395\t**The current activation (`act` only; a `tail` skips A1-A6).**\n396\t\n397\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n398\t|---|---|---|\n399\t| A1 | `inst(pc−1)` is one of `:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation`, or a `:call` with `tail?` false | `:site` |\n400\t| A2 | `(:yin.k/rd deliver) = (code/rd inst(pc−1))` and `deliver = {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}` exactly | `:destination` / `:deliver` |\n401\t| A3 | every window key `< k(body(pc))` | `:window-scope` |\n402\t| A4 | `rd ∉ (keys window)` | `:window-self` |\n403\t| A5 | `(set (keys window)) ⊆ (analysis/saved a pc rd)` | `:window-extra` |\n404\t| A6 | `(analysis/saved a pc rd) ⊆ (set (keys window))` | `:window-missing` |\n405\t\n406\t**The tail site (`tail` only).**\n407\t\n408\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n409\t|---|---|---|\n410\t| T1 | `inst(site)` is a `:call` with `tail?` true; `deliver = {:yin.k/deliver :return}` exactly | `:site` / `:deliver` |\n411\t\n412\t**Return frames (every frame, each `K[i]`).**\n413\t\n414\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n415\t|---|---|---|\n416\t| K1 | `inst(frame.pc−1)` in `frame.segment` is a `:call` with `tail?` false | `:return-site` |\n417\t| K2 | `frame.rd = (code/rd inst(frame.pc−1))` | `:return-destination` |\n418\t| K3 | every key of `frame.window` `< k(body(frame.pc))`; `frame.rd ∉ keys`; `(set (keys frame.window)) = (analysis/saved aₛ frame.pc frame.rd)` in that segment's analysis, reported as `:return-window-scope` / `:return-window-self` / `:return-window-extra` / `:return-window-missing` | as named |\n419\t\n420\t**Environments (every `E`: the act's and each return frame's).**\n421\t\n422\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n423\t|---|---|---|\n424\t| E1 | no binding key is reserved (`vm/reserved-name?`) | `:reserved-name` |\n425\t| E2 | **`E` is the free environment under the lexical chain.** With `C = (set (mapcat params (chain (body pc))))` the lexically bound names at that pc (a return frame's chain is that of its own `pc`): (a) **every name of `C` is a key of `bindings`**, a nil binding being a present key (`bind-params` nil-fills, so a lexical name is never absent; an absent one would be nil-filled on lower and break the canonical round trip); (b) `(apply dissoc bindings C) = (apply dissoc (:free-env task) C)` as maps of `V`: a key outside `C` the free environment lacks, a free key `E` lacks, or a differing value, is a defect | `:free-env-mismatch` |\n426\t\n427\t**Task (once per body, before any frame; `frame/task-defect`).**\n428\t\n429\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n430\t|---|---|---|\n431\t| X1 | the root's `free-env` decodes to a map `{S V …}` with symbol keys, sorted and unique on the wire, none reserved (`vm/reserved-name?`, the same check `create-vm` runs on its `:env`, `vm/check-bindings!`, `vm.cljc:161-168`); it is the `:free-env` every E2 compares against | `:free-env-shape` / `:reserved-name` (path `[:free-env]`) |\n432\t\n433\t**Pending and contexts.**\n434\t\n435\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n436\t|---|---|---|\n437\t| P1 | `context :wait` requires `:yin.k/pending` present; `:captured` and `:parked` require it absent | `:pending-presence` |\n438\t| P2 | (`:wait`) the pending's reason is admitted by `inst(pc−1)` (act) or `inst(site)` (tail): `:stream-next → :next`; `:stream-put → :put`; `:ffi-call → :ffi, :ffi-request`; `:call → :next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request :link-response :install`; `:park` and `:current-continuation` admit none (frozen §4.1; `wire-of-kind`, `handoff.cljc:491-504`; ucf-revisions §8) | `:reason-mismatch` |\n439\t| P3 | (`:captured`) the frame is an `act` and `inst(pc−1)` is `:current-continuation` | `:captured-site` |\n440\t| P4 | (`:parked`) either `inst(pc−1)` is `:park` (an `act`), or `(:parked-key task) ∈ (:ffi-call-ids task)` and the frame is an `act` with `inst(pc−1) = :ffi-call` or a `tail` whose `inst(site)` is a tail `:call` (bookkeeping, evaluator §1.6) | `:parked-site` |\n441\t\n442\tRule E2 is the correction the review required (§4.4): a merged `E`\n443\tcannot be split into free and lexical parts without the task's free\n444\tenvironment, so the carrier carries it and the validator checks that\n445\tevery `E` agrees with it off the lexical names. Nothing here reads a\n446\tstack depth, a stack base or a `live` vector.\n447\t\n448\t### 3.3 Where and when the validator runs\n449\t\n450\t**DECIDED** (carrier order kept: draft §8, step 7 after step 6; the\n451\treview's ruling). On **lower**: gate (1), structure (2), graph (3),\n452\thashes (4), canonical form (5), canonical numbering (6), **frames (7)**,\n453\tpending and operations (8), dependency closure (9), custody (10),\n454\tcomposition (11), restore (12). In step 7 the body's decoded task\n455\tcontext is judged first by `frame/task-defect` (X1), then every `:act`,\n456\t`:tail`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and parked-table state is decoded and\n457\tjudged by `frame/defect` with its context and that task context; a defect\n458\tis `:yin.k/undecodable` with the rule's kind and the row path. A\n459\tfirst-defect test therefore sees a numbering defect\n460\t(`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`) before a frame defect on a body that has both,\n461\tand §8's acceptance items say so. On **lift**: the same function runs\n462\tover every assembled frame before any row is emitted; a frame the\n463\tevaluator itself minted that fails is `:yin.k/non-portable`\n464\t`:inconsistent-state`. The harness runs it over every frame a layout\n465\treconstructs.\n466\t\n467\t---\n468\t\n469\t## 4. Layouts: reconstructing the canonical frame\n470\t\n471\t### 4.1 What a layout is\n472\t\n473\t**DECIDED** (frozen §4.3; UCF §7.4.2). A foreign engine publishes a\n474\tstatic map per segment and engine (`A`, resume pcs, `def`/`use`/`L`\n475\tfrom `analysis/analyze`, `lexically-required`, and a layout from\n476\tphysical location to virtual register) and reconstructs the canonical\n477\tframe from its state at a safepoint; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` on\n478\tadmitted canonical frames. Two kernels are intended to reconstruct\n479\tcanonical frames: the de Bruijn register kernel over `R(A)` and the de\n480\tBruijn stack kernel over `H(A)` (phase 5). The AST walker is not (frozen\n481\t§10 phase 6 names the three kernels; the walker has no pc).\n482\t\n483\t**PROPOSED** namespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout`, file\n484\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, requiring\n485\t`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `.derive` (phase 5),\n486\t`yin.vm.debruijn` (`resolve-name`), `yin.vm.engine` (`bind-params`), and\n487\tthe two kernels' public state. Public: `register-layout`,\n488\t`stack-layout` (static, per segment), `lift-act`, `lift-tail`,\n489\t`lower-act`, `lower-tail` (per kernel, dispatching on the kernel's\n490\t`link-format`).\n491\t\n492\t### 4.2 The register kernel's layout\n493\t\n494\t**DECIDED** by phase-5 §2.4 (R's bodies and pcs are A's) and §2.3\n495\t(`R(v) = locals + slot(v)`); the kernel's payload shape from\n496\t`debruijn/register.cljc:690-704` (call frame: `:site-pc :return-pc\n497\t:frames :regs :live :dest :store-of`) and `write-back` (`:455-485`:\n498\t`:write-result` rebuilds a dense register vector from `regs` and writes\n499\t`dest`; `:return-result` runs the return transition).\n500\t\n501\t**Static half.** For segment `A` with `R = (:image (derive/register-image\n502\tv))`: for every pc `p`, `v[p]` names virtual registers in its `rd`,\n503\t`:reg` and `:regs` slots and `R[p]` names physical registers in the same\n504\tslots. `phys(body, v)` is read off any pc that names `v`; it is total on\n505\tthe virtuals a body names. Its inverse is **restricted**: `virt(p, s)`\n506\tis defined only for `s` in the physical image of\n507\t`live-out*(p) = ⋃ L(q) for q ∈ (analysis/successors v p)` (the actual\n508\tsuccessors, phase-5 §2.5; empty for a tail call) at a **reachable**\n509\tboundary pc `p` (`analysis/reachable`); there it is \"the `v ∈ live-out*(p)`\n510\twith `phys(v) = s`\", single-valued because the allocator's intervals\n511\tcover every live point and distinct live virtuals hold distinct slots\n512\t(a property the harness checks, §5.6 item 3, never assumed). Physical\n513\toperands of **unreachable** layout-syntax instructions (the structural\n514\tslot shared by several definition-less ids, phase-5 §2.2) are not\n515\tinverted; they are validated separately by the kernel's own\n516\t`register-image-defect` and by phase-5's gates, and never reach a\n517\tcanonical frame (no reachable instruction reads them).\n518\t\n519\t**Lift, `act`.** From the kernel's wait entry with `:resume-mode\n520\t:write-result` (payload `:image :site-pc :pc :frames :regs :live\n521\t:continuation :dest :store-of`, v2 amendment §5.3):\n522\t\n523\t1. `A` = the A the image row of the entry's `:image` was derived from\n524\t   (the kernel's code space is keyed by A after phase 5; R is its\n525\t   checksum).\n526\t2. relative `pc` = the entry's `pc` minus the image row's offset\n527\t   (`image-row`/`absolute-pc`, `debruijn/register.cljc:326-337`); R's pcs\n528\t   are A's, so this is the canonical pc.\n529\t3. `rd = (code/rd (nth v (dec pc)))` **from A**, never the physical\n530\t   `:dest` (which is `phys(rd)`).\n531\t4. `W = {virt(pc−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ regs}`; the kernel's `live` is\n532\t   `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(pc−1) rd)))` (the subtraction is on\n533\t   **virtual** ids before mapping: on the worked example `live-out*(3) =\n534\t   L(4) = {1 2}`, `(disj … 2) = {1}`, `phys → [0]`; subtracting the\n535\t   physical `3` from `{1 2}` would wrongly keep `2`), so `(keys W) =\n536\t   saved(pc, rd)`, which rule A5/A6 then checks.\n537\t5. `E` = §4.4 from the entry's `frames` and `store-of`, at `body(pc)`.\n538\t6. `K`: for each continuation frame `f`, **independently**: its\n539\t   `return-pc` is absolute in the kernel's concatenated code space, so\n540\t   first its image row `rowₖ = (image-row images return-pc)` and relative\n541\t   `pcₖ = return-pc − offsetₖ`, then `Aₖ` of that row (frames may cross\n542\t   images: a module closure called from another image returns into its\n543\t   caller's image), `rdₖ = (code/rd (nth vₖ (dec pcₖ)))` from `Aₖ`, never\n544\t   `f.dest`; `Wₖ = {virt_{Aₖ}(pcₖ−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ f.regs}`; `Eₖ` = §4.4\n545\t   from `f.frames` and `f.store-of` at `body(pcₖ)` in `Aₖ`.\n546\t7. pending: the engine fields of the entry, as `lift-pending!` maps them\n547\t   (`handoff.cljc:674-749`).\n548\t\n549\t**Lift, `tail`.** From an entry with `:resume-mode :return-result`\n550\t(`dest nil`, `live []`): `A` and the relative `site = site-pc` as in 1-2\n551\t(the entry's `site-pc` is the tail call; its `pc` is `site+1`); `K` as\n552\tin 6; pending as in 7; **no** `W`, `E` or `pc` are read: the entry's\n553\t`regs` (empty by `live []`) and `frames` are discarded (frozen §2.4: a\n554\ttail state carries no window and no `E`).\n555\t\n556\t**Lower, `act`.** Given a canonical `act` and the receiver's attached\n557\timage for `A` (derived by `derive/register-image` from the carried\n558\tvector, offset `off`): `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + pc − 1`;\n559\t`:pc = off + pc`; `:regs = (sort-by first [[phys(v) V] for [v V] in W])`;\n560\t`:live = (sort (map phys (keys W)))` (the kernel's `live-exact` rule\n561\trecomputes `body-liveness` and must agree); `:dest = phys(rd)`;\n562\t`:resume-mode :write-result`; `:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of`\n563\tfrom `E`; `:continuation` = for each `K[i]`, with its own `Aₖ`'s image\n564\trow `offₖ`: `{:site-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ −1) :return-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ) :regs\n565\t… :live … :dest (phys_{Aₖ} rdₖ) :frames (§4.4 from Eₖ) :store-of …}`;\n566\t`:format reffects/format-tag`, `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's current\n567\taggregate (never carried). Engine fields from the pending\n568\t(`lower-frame!`, `handoff.cljc:3182-3246`), `:deliver` carried.\n569\t\n570\t**Lower, `tail`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + site`;\n571\t`:pc = off + site + 1`; `:regs []`; `:live []`; `:dest nil`;\n572\t`:resume-mode :return-result`; `:frames []` and `:store-of nil`\n573\t(unused: `write-back`'s `:return-result` branch reads `:frames` and\n574\t`:store-of` only to assign them before the return transition replaces\n575\tthem from the popped frame, `register.cljc:461-468`; the harness checks\n576\tthat the values chosen here are never observable, §5.6 item 8);\n577\t`:continuation` as for `act`. The register kernel already supports this\n578\tdelivery (`:return-result`, `register.cljc:458-468`).\n579\t\n580\t### 4.3 The stack kernel's layout\n581\t\n582\t**DECIDED** by phase-5 §3.2 (the emitter's walk) and the kernel's call\n583\tand return arms (`debruijn/stack.cljc:716-776`): a non-tail `:call`\n584\tpops `f` and the `argc` operands, leaving `stack'`, and pushes\n585\t`{:return-pc (inc pc) :frames frames :stack-base (count stack')}`\n586\t(`:728-733`); `:return` truncates to the frame's `stack-base` and pushes\n587\tthe value (`:766-772`); a host-function effect parks with the\n588\tcontinuation after the call site and `stack-restore` resumes by conjing\n589\tthe value at the entry's `pc` (`:748-752`, `:555-568`).\n590\t\n591\t**Static half.**\n592\t\n593\t1. **pc correspondence.** The stack emitter visits parse nodes in\n594\t   `project`'s order and emits one instruction per A instruction plus a\n595\t   `:push` after each child of a call, each `:stream-put` target and\n596\t   each `:ffi-call` operand (phase-5 §3.2). So H **with every `:push`\n597\t   removed** corresponds one-to-one, in order, with A: the k-th non-push\n598\t   H instruction ↔ A pc `k`; `h->a`, `a->h` are the two directions. The\n599\t   harness checks mnemonic families agree at every k (§5.6 item 4).\n600\t2. **operand layout.** For an A pc `p` and the parse node at `p`,\n601\t   `stack-layout(p)` is the vector, bottom to top, of the virtual\n602\t   registers whose values are pushed and not yet consumed while control\n603\t   is inside `p`'s expression: for each ancestor `n` and the child `c` on\n604\t   the path to `p`, the children of `n` before `c` that `n` pushes (a\n605\t   `:call`: the operator and the operands before `c`; a `:stream-put`:\n606\t   the target when `c` is the value; an `:ffi-call`: the operands before\n607\t   `c`; `:if`, `:define`, `:stream-cursor/next/close`, `:resume`: none).\n608\t   **Checked** (§5.6 item 5): for a **non-tail** parking instruction,\n609\t   `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p) (code/rd v[p]))`.\n610\t   For a **tail** `:call` the canonical state has no window and\n611\t   `stack-layout(p)` is not consulted (it may be non-empty for a tail\n612\t   call in operand position, whose pushed siblings are dead layout\n613\t   syntax).\n614\t\n615\t**K slices (corrected).** Let the kernel's continuation be\n616\t`[f₀ … f_{m−1}]` outermost first with bases `b₀ … b_{m−1}` and the\n617\toperand stack `St` of length `n`. Because `bᵢ = (count stack')` is the\n618\tcaller's residual height **after** popping the callee's operands, the\n619\tslice of operands belonging to activation `i` is\n620\t\n621\t```\n622\tslice(f₀)      = St[0, b₀)\n623\tslice(fᵢ)      = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)        for 0 < i < m\n624\tslice(current) = St[b_{m−1}, n)         ; or St[0, n) when K is empty\n625\t```\n626\t\n627\t(the review's correction: `[previous-base, base_i)` with previous-base\n628\t0, and the current activation `[last-base, count)`).\n629\t\n630\t**Lift, `act`.** From a wait entry `{:image :pc :frames :stack\n631\t:continuation :store-of}` (v2 amendment §5.2) whose `pc−1` in H is **not**\n632\ta tail `:call`:\n633\t\n634\t1. `A` from the image row; relative H pc `pcₕ = pc − off`; canonical\n635\t   `pc = h->a(pcₕ − 1) + 1`; `rd = (code/rd v[pc−1])`.\n636\t2. `W = (zipmap (stack-layout (dec pc)) slice(current))`; the two have\n637\t   equal length (checked) and `(keys W) = saved(pc, rd)` (rule A5/A6).\n638\t3. `E` = §4.4 from `frames` and `store-of` at `body(pc)`.\n639\t4. `K`: for `fᵢ` **independently**: its `return-pc` is absolute; row and\n640\t   relative `pcₕᵢ` through the stack kernel's own offset table\n641\t   (`debruijn/stack.cljc:355`, `absolute-pc`'s inverse); `Aᵢ` of that row;\n642\t   `pcᵢ = h->a_{Aᵢ}(pcₕᵢ − 1) + 1`; `rdᵢ = (code/rd vᵢ[pcᵢ−1])`;\n643\t   `Wᵢ = (zipmap (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) slice(fᵢ))`; `Eᵢ` = §4.4\n644\t   from `fᵢ.frames` and `fᵢ.store-of`.\n645\t5. pending from the engine fields.\n646\t\n647\t**Lift, `tail`.** From a wait entry whose `pc−1` in H is a tail `:call`:\n648\t`A` and `site = h->a(pcₕ − 1)`; `K` as in 4; pending; the entry's\n649\t`frames`, `store-of` and `slice(current)` are **discarded** (dead\n650\tlayout syntax; frozen §2.4).\n651\t\n652\t**Lower, `act`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:pc = off + a->h(pc−1) + 1`;\n653\t`:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of` from `E`; `:stack` = the\n654\tconcatenation over `K` outermost first of each `slice(fᵢ)` =\n655\t`(mapv Wᵢ (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)))`, then `slice(current) =\n656\t(mapv W (stack-layout (dec pc)))`; `:continuation` = for each `fᵢ`\n657\t`{:return-pc (+ offᵢ (a->h_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) 1) :frames (§4.4 from Eᵢ)\n658\t:stack-base bᵢ :store-of …}` where **`bᵢ` is the cumulative length of\n659\tthe slices up to and including `slice(fᵢ)`**: `b₀ = |slice(f₀)|`, `bᵢ =\n660\tb_{i−1} + |slice(fᵢ)|`. This is the inverse of the lift's\n661\t`slice(fᵢ) = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)`: the kernel's base is the caller's\n662\tresidual height, which is exactly the end of the caller's own slice\n663\t(`stack.cljc:731-733`). On the multi-frame fixture of §8.3 item 6\n664\t(five return frames, slices `[0,0) [0,0) [0,0) [0,2) [2,4)`) this emits\n665\tbases `0, 0, 0, 2, 4`; the before-slice rule would emit `0, 0, 0, 0, 2`\n666\tand the kernel would truncate to the wrong height on return. `:format\n667\tstack/format-tag`; `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's. `stack-restore`\n668\tthen conjes the delivered value onto `:stack` at `:pc`, the `:push`-free\n669\tposition after the parking instruction, exactly where H expects the\n670\tresult.\n671\t\n672\t**Lower, `tail`, and the tail-completion seam.** Today the stack kernel\n673\tresumes a parked host effect at the entry's `pc` by pushing the value\n674\t(`stack-restore`, `:566`); for a **tail** call that resumes the body at\n675\t`a->h(site)+1`, which then runs the layout syntax after the tail call\n676\t(for a tail call in operand position: the remaining pushes and the\n677\tenclosing call) **before** reaching `:return`. That is not the canonical\n678\tthrough-return delivery (frozen §2.4, §4.1 \"effectful call, tail\":\n679\tdelivery **through return** against `K`). The layout therefore cannot\n680\tlower a canonical `tail` into today's stack kernel. **PROPOSED, gated\n681\t(§8.3 slice 6c-pre):** the stack kernel gains a tail-completion seam:\n682\tits park-entry builders mark an entry whose parking instruction is a\n683\ttail `:call` with `:resume-mode :return-result` (the register kernel's\n684\texisting vocabulary, `register.cljc:458-468`), and `stack-restore`\n685\tdelivers such an entry by the **return transition** (pop the top frame,\n686\ttruncate `:stack` to its base, push the value, restore its `frames`,\n687\t`store-of` and `return-pc`; halt with the value when `K` is empty) instead\n688\tof resuming at `pc`; the retained-FFI re-park and the response decoding\n689\t(`stack-restore`'s `:request-sent` and `:call-id` steps, `:548-557`) keep\n690\ttheir order before the final delivery, as in the register kernel.\n691\tLowering a canonical `tail` then produces\n692\t`{:image row-of-A :pc (+ off (a->h site) 1) :resume-mode :return-result\n693\t:stack (concat of the K slices, in order) :frames [] :store-of nil\n694\t:continuation …}` with each continuation frame's `:stack-base bᵢ` the\n695\t**same cumulative-including rule as the `act` lowering** (the last\n696\tframe's base equals the stack's length, since the current activation\n697\tcontributes no slice); the `:frames`/`:store-of` values are unobservable\n698\t(the return transition replaces them) and the harness checks that (§5.6\n699\titem 8). File:\n700\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc` (not a 4b or phase-5 file); the\n701\tregister kernel needs no change.\n702\t\n703\t### 4.4 Positional lexical frames, the free environment and `E`\n704\t\n705\t**DECIDED** by frozen §2.1 and §2.4 (`E_c[ps ↦ a]` by `bind-params`),\n706\tphase-5 §1.2 (the owner chain), and the kernels' resolution of free\n707\tnames through the task-wide `free-env` (`stack.cljc:683`:\n708\t`(engine/resolve-var free-env …)`). Both de Bruijn kernels hold the\n709\tlexical environment as positional frames `F = [[V …] …]` outermost first,\n710\tone per enclosing closure activation, plus the task-wide `free-env`.\n711\t\n712\t**Forward (F → E), lossless.** At a pc of body `b` with\n713\t`chain = [c₀ … c_{j}]` outermost first (phase-5's `chain` reversed):\n714\t\n715\t```\n716\tE = (merge free-env\n717\t           (bind-params params(c₀) F[0])\n718\t           …\n719\t           (bind-params params(c_j) F[j]))\n720\t    ∪ {engine/store-of-key M}  when the activation's closure carries store-of M\n721\t```\n722\t\n723\tThis reproduces the register evaluator's `E` on closure entry\n724\t(`semantic_register.cljc:141-143`: `(merge (:env c) (bind-params\n725\t(:params c) args))`, `(:env c)` being the creating activation's `E`,\n726\titself `free-env` merged with the outer chain), including rightmost-wins\n727\tduplicates, nil-fill and extra-argument dropping, which are\n728\t`engine/bind-params`'s.\n729\t\n730\t**Inverse (E → F), and why the free environment travels.** The reviewer's\n731\tcounterexample, with initial free `x = 99`:\n732\t\n733\t```clojure\n734\t(let [f (fn [] x)]\n735\t  ((fn [x] (f (stream-next c))) 1))\n736\t```\n737\t\n738\tAt the blocked read the current `E` binds `x` to 1 (the parameter). The\n739\tearlier revision recovered the receiver's free environment as \"`E` minus\n740\tthe chain's names\", which drops `x` entirely, and the lowered de Bruijn\n741\tclosure `f`, whose body is `[:load-free x]`, resolves `x` through the\n742\ttask-wide `free-env` and loses 99. **So the task's free environment is\n743\tcarried explicitly** (root slot `free-env`, §2.6; §6.3), every `E` is\n744\tchecked against it off the lexical names (rule E2), and the inverse is:\n745\t\n746\t```\n747\tfree-env  := the root's free-env row (never derived from any E)\n748\tF[i]      := (mapv #(get E %) params(cᵢ))      for i = 0 … j\n749\t```\n750\t\n751\t`F[i]` for an **outer** frame whose parameter is shadowed by an inner\n752\tframe receives the inner value, not the outer one; that value is\n753\tunobservable from `body(pc)` (resolution picks the innermost binder,\n754\t`resolve-name`, `debruijn.cljc:617-637`), and the canonical frame does\n755\tnot carry it (frozen §2.1: parameters live in `E`, and `E` holds one\n756\tvalue per name). The round-trip law is therefore stated on **canonical\n757\tframes** (`lift(lower(frame)) = frame`), not on kernel state, and the\n758\tharness's `E` check (§5.6 item 6) compares the layout's `E` with the\n759\tevaluator's; shadowed outer positional values are not a claim of this\n760\tdesign. The free bindings that *are* observable, by any closure's\n761\t`:load-free`, are preserved exactly by the root slot.\n762\t\n763\t**Closure values.** A de Bruijn closure (positional marker: segment,\n764\tentry, arity, frames `F`, store-of) reconstructs to the canonical\n765\tclosure (`{:type :closure :params :entry :segment :env}`, evaluator\n766\t§1.8): `params` from A's `:closure` tuple at the owner pc of the entry\n767\tbody, `env` by the forward rule over the closure's captured `F` and the\n768\ttask's `free-env`, `entry` the body pc. The inverse reads `F` back from\n769\t`env` by the chain. Checked both ways (§5.6 item 7).\n770\t\n771\t### 4.5 What a layout never does\n772\t\n773\t**DECIDED.** A layout reads a kernel's state, A and the carried task\n774\tcontext; it never runs code, never fills a binding from the receiver's\n775\tstore or free environment (the free environment is the **carried** one,\n776\t§6.3), never reads a native image's hash as an identity, and never\n777\tinvents a safepoint: a kernel pc whose predecessor is not a parking\n778\tinstruction is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2).\n779\t\n780\t---\n781\t\n782\t## 5. The safepoint harness\n783\t\n784\t### 5.1 What it proves\n785\t\n786\t**DECIDED** (frozen §10 phase 6 gate; UCF §7.11). For every row of the\n787\texpanded matrix, both delivery modes, nested and reified captures,\n788\tmodule contexts and every refusal: lift at the safepoint, lower into a\n789\tfresh machine, continue, and result and effect trace equal the\n790\tuninterrupted reference run; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame`; the frame's\n791\treason is the observed wait's; on the three lanes: `:semantic-register`,\n792\t`:stack-through-layout`, `:register-through-layout`.\n793\t\n794\t### 5.2 The matrix\n795\t\n796\t**Expected results are stated for the bare expression.** Each row runs\n797\tunder the four segment wrappers of the old harness\n798\t(`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:92-106`), whose expected results follow\n799\tmechanically from the bare result `r`: `:bare` → `r`; `:operand` →\n800\t`[:operand r]` (`(cat [:operand] r)`); `:depth-3` → `[3 [2 [1 r]]]`\n801\t(`nest` wraps `(cat [d] ((fn [x] …) d))` three times, as the old test's\n802\tpinned `[2 [1 [:two 2 \"C\"]]]` shows for depth 2, `:574`); `:captured` →\n803\t`[:captured :arg r]` (`(cat (cat (cat [] k) y) r)` with `k = :captured`,\n804\t`y = :arg`). **Frames the wrappers add.** The harness marks `:tail?\n805\ttrue` only where a row says so (`:228`, `:256`; `ast->semantic-bytecode`\n806\tdefaults it to false, `vm.cljc:1089`; `mark-tail` is the expander's and\n807\tis not run here), so every `let1` and every `nest` application is a\n808\tnon-tail call that pushes a return frame. `with-cat` (`:69-76`,\n809\toutermost in every segment, `:96`) adds **one** frame below everything\n810\tthe row builds, with `stack-base 0` and an empty slice on the stack lane\n811\t(nothing is pushed before its application). On top of it `:bare` and\n812\t`:operand` add none of their own (the row is the operand), `:depth-3`\n813\tadds three (`nest`'s applications, each with two values, `cat` and\n814\t`[d]`, pushed under it), and `:captured` adds three (`let1 k`, `let1 f`,\n815\t`(f :arg)`). A row's own `let1`s add theirs. Row 20 alone runs outside\n816\tthe wrappers.\n817\t\n818\tPrograms use `v2_support`'s AST helpers (`v2_support.cljc:56-104`:\n819\t`lit v lam app def! then cursor-of next-of let1`) and the four segments\n820\tof the old harness (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:92-106`: `:bare`,\n821\t`:operand`, `:depth-3`, `:captured`); `cat` is the closure `(fn [p q]\n822\t(conj p q))` bound around every body (`:69-81`). \"Expected\" is the\n823\treference run's normalised result; where a row says \"executable\" it runs\n824\ton all three lanes; \"synthetic\" means the frame is built by hand and\n825\tjudged by `frame/defect` only, because no program reaches it on the\n826\tlanded or stage-2 evaluator.\n827\t\n828\t| # | Row | Program (inside each segment) | How parked / satisfied | State, pending | Expected after satisfy | Executable? |\n829\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n830\t| 1 | blocked read | `(let1 s (stream/make 4) (let1 c (cursor-of s) (let1 a (next-of c) (cat (cat [] a) (next-of c)))))`, `\"A\"` appended before the run | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next` | `[\"A\" \"B\"]` | yes |\n831\t| 2 | blocked write | `(stream-put w \"v\")` on a one-slot stream warmed with `:warmed` | take one | act, `:put` with retained `\"v\"` | `\"v\"` landed | yes |\n832\t| 3 | FFI sent | `(dao.stream.apply/call :op/echo \"hello\")` | append the success response | act, `:ffi` | `\"echo!\"` | 4b stage 2 |\n833\t| 4 | FFI retained | as 3 with a one-slot, warmed call-in | free the slot, then answer | act, `:ffi-request` | `\"echo!\"` | 4b stage 2 |\n834\t| 5 | effectful call, non-tail, stream | `(cat [:ordinary] (stream/next! c))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next` | `[:ordinary \"B\"]` | yes (stage 1 has streams; `stream/next!` is the stream module's) |\n835\t| 6 | **effectful call, tail, stream** | `((fn [u] (stream/next! c)) 0)` with the inner call `:tail? true` | append `\"B\"` | **tail**, `:next` | `\"B\"` delivered through `K` | yes |\n836\t| 7 | link request phase | `(require 'host.mod)` parked **before** the request append succeeds (one-slot, warmed request stream) | free the slot, then answer `:ok` | act, `:link-request` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n837\t| 8 | link response phase | `(require 'host.mod)` polling (request appended) | append the `:ok` response | act, `:link-response` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n838\t| 9 | **link, tail** | `((fn [u] (require 'host.mod)) 0)` tail | as 8 | **tail**, `:link-response` | `'host.mod` through `K` | 4b stage 2 |\n839\t| 10 | install, child running | `(require 'host.mod)` where `host.mod`'s body reads a stream the test has not fed, so the child is `:blocked` and the parent's wait is `:install` | feed the child's stream | act, `:install` + install entry, child kind `:blocked`, phase `:running` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n840\t| 11 | install, child parked | as 10 with the child body executing `:park` | resume the child by id | act, `:install`, child kind `:parked`, phase `:parked` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n841\t| 12 | install, tail | `((fn [u] (require 'host.mod)) 0)` tail with 10's child | as 10 | **tail**, `:install` | `'host.mod` through `K` | 4b stage 2 |\n842\t| 13 | explicit park | `(vm/park)` | `(vm/resume pid \"back\")` beside | parked record, root `:parked` | `\"back\"` | yes |\n843\t| 14 | reified capture live in a window | `(let1 k (vm/current-continuation) (cat (cat [] k) (next-of c)))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `k` is a `:reified` value in `W` | `[:continuation \"B\"]` normalised | yes |\n844\t| 15 | capture invoked after lower | `(let1 k (vm/current-continuation) (if (= k :second) :done (let1 r (next-of c) (k :second))))`: on the first pass `k` is a continuation, so the test fails and the read runs and parks; after satisfaction `(k :second)` delivers `:second` into the capture's `rd` (the `let1` operand) and re-enters at the capture's successor pc, so the body is entered again with `k = :second` and the test succeeds **without repeating the read** (the invocation is abortive on every lane: `:continuation` operator kind, `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:212-217` stage 2, `stack.cljc:760-763`). `if` is `{:type :if :test :consequent :alternate}` (`vm.cljc:1069`), a helper the new harness file adds; `=` is the primitive (`vm.cljc:387`), consumed before the read | append `\"x\"` after lower | act, `:next`; `k` is a `:reified` value in `E` (the closure's) | `:done` | yes |\n845\t| 16 | nested capture | `(let1 k1 (vm/current-continuation) (let1 k2 (vm/current-continuation) (cat (cat (cat [] k1) k2) (next-of c))))` | append `\"B\"` | act; `k2`'s act window holds `k1` (a `:reified` inside a `:reified`'s window) | `[:continuation :continuation \"B\"]` | yes |\n846\t| 17 | module context, blocked inside a module closure | `host.mod` exports `f = (fn [c] (stream/next! c))`; root: `(f c)` after `(require 'host.mod)` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`, `E` carries `store-of host.mod` | `\"B\"` | 4b stage 2 (link) |\n847\t| 18 | module-store snapshot | `parked-module-store-cursors` (`lift_support.cljc:310-333`): an explicit park whose module store holds two cursors named by a module closure | resume | parked record; `:module-store` row | the two cursor cells | yes |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"02df6bdc-04b9-48af-9b17-9798293c08e5","uuid":"c3227a7f-3e63-470f-aa95-50e6b0c09274","timestamp":"2026-10-12T17:42:00.043Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-docs-6b-amend/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"# yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation: phase 6, the continuation format and the row carrier\n\nStatus: **Companion design, phase 6 of `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`\n§10.** Written 2026-10-11 (Architect: claude-fable-5-1) from the frozen\ndesign as amended (`8f0b9e38`), the landed 4a evaluator\n(`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc`) and the 4b stage-1 evaluator\non branch `srvm-phase4b` (read only), the merged phase-5 derivations\ndesign, the UCF document and its version-2 amendment, the row-carrier\ndraft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`, body version 3, never landed),\nthe two de Bruijn kernels, and the landed UCF code and harness\n(`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc`,\n`test/yin/vm/ucf/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`). Revision 2 (2026-10-11)\napplies the codex review\n`collab/1791760000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n(CHANGES_REQUESTED): the task's free environment is carried explicitly\n(§4.4, §6.3), the stack kernel's K slices and tail completion are\ncorrected and gated (§4.3, §8.3), register return destinations are\nrecovered from A (§4.2), `act` and `tail` have separate algorithms, the\nvalidator has a complete pure input contract and the carrier's order\n(§3), the static laws use actual-successor live-out (§5.6), blinding is\na specified re-emission (§5.5), the harness matrix is expanded (§5.2),\n6a is the independent codec (§8.1), and the amendment inventory is\nexhaustive (§10). Revision 3 (2026-10-11) applies the codex round-2\nreview\n`collab/1791800000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n(CHANGES_REQUESTED): stack lowering bases are cumulative through each\nslice (§4.3), the live operand subtracts the virtual destination before\nmapping (§4.2, §5.6), the free environment's source on lift is specified\nwith its owner (§6.3), E2 requires lexical presence and X1 validates the\nroot's free environment (§3.2), the harness oracles of rows 15, 20, 22\nand 23 and the segment-wrapper results are corrected (§5.2), the\ncross-lane re-emission takes an explicit stream correspondence (§5.5),\nand the amendment residue is closed (§10.3 R9, U7). The frozen document\nis not changed by this one.\nSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md), including its\nunitarity invariant, and to\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md)\n(\"the frozen design\"); format and rigor model:\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md)\nand [`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md).\n\nEvery section marks its content **DECIDED** (an owner ruling, frozen\ntext, or code that exists) or **PROPOSED** (a design choice of this\ndocument, open to the reviewer). File references are to the worktree at\n`8f0b9e38`; line numbers are those of that commit; the 4b branch is\ncited as `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc`, with the line numbers of\nits stage-1 commit (`0f4522b9`) unless a citation says stage 2\n(`bdf48cf4`, committed after revision 2; stage 2 moved the reified\npayload to `:256`, `create-vm` to `:349-375` and `gc-roots`/`gc-children`\nto `:485-488`, and changed none of the shapes this document reads).\n\nContents: §0 rulings and the one-paragraph answer · §1 the canonical\nframe · §2 the row carrier · §3 the frame validator · §4 layouts · §5\nthe safepoint harness · §6 code and task context in the carrier · §7\nlift and lower · §8 slices, gates and phase 7's entry conditions · §9\nverification honesty · §10 findings and amendments · §11 owner\nquestions · §12 portability.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Rulings and the one-paragraph answer\n\n### 0.1 Rulings that bind this design (DECIDED)\n\n1. **A is the only code identity**; new identities digest under Jing's\n   default (BLAKE3), never SHA-256 (`ucf/code-address` is\n   `jing/segment-key`, `ucf.cljc:247-251`).\n2. **No legacy**: one body version, no reader for older bodies, no\n   compat shim, no migration path; clean breaks.\n3. **Unitarity**: the AST is never lost and is always retrievable\n   wherever code is lowered; no design assumes a tree-less receiver; the\n   segment-to-tree association of the phase-5 design (§9.2 there) is a\n   given, including its completeness rule: an absent entry **or an empty\n   tree set** is incomplete.\n4. **The vector-to-datoms mapping is retired**; the carrier carries\n   positional tuples and rows, never datoms-from-vector.\n5. **dao.stream is the complexity boundary**: no transport mechanics.\n6. **One truth, many interpretations**: the register machine's\n   configuration `C = ⟨seg, pc, W⟩` with `E`, `K` is the canonical frame\n   shape; the stack and register kernels reconstruct it through their\n   layouts (frozen §4.3, §10 phase 6).\n7. **No file of phase 4b or phase 5 is touched**, so this phase runs\n   concurrently with them (§8.5 names the dependencies that remain, and\n   the one old-kernel file 6c must change).\n\n### 0.2 The answer\n\nA continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register\nmachine, `act` or `tail`, with its saved window, its `K` of `:return`\nframes, its environment, its delivery record and its pending wait\n(frozen §2.4, §4.2), plus the task context it needs: the task's **free\nenvironment**, store slice, module stores, parked table, cursor cells, code by\nA and the tree association. **§1** restates the frame as the 4b\nevaluator mints it. **§2** gives the row carrier: body version 3, one\nprofile (`\"v4\"`), Merkle content rows under Jing's default digest, the\nwindow as a sorted keyed row in which a nil value is a present\n`[:lit nil]`, `:act`, `:tail`, `:window`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and\n`:parked-table` rows replacing the stale stack-shaped rows, a root\n`free-env` slot, the pending rows and census unchanged. **§3** gives the\nvalidator as an ordered set of rules over the decoded frame, the carried\ncode and decoded task context, engine-independent, run after canonical\nnumbering as the carrier orders it. **§4** defines the two layouts with\nseparate `act` and `tail` algorithms: the register kernel's physical\nregisters are paired with A's virtual ones pc by pc and its return\ndestinations recovered from A's call; the stack kernel's operand stack\nis sliced `[previous-base, base_i)` per frame, and its tail completion is\na kernel seam this phase must add and gate. **§5** is the harness over\nthree lanes with an expanded, concrete matrix and a specified\nre-emission procedure for cross-lane equality. **§6** fixes what travels\nfor code and task context. **§8** cuts the slices: 6a the independent\nframe and row codec, 6b full task transport (needs phase 5's scanners\nand 4b stage 2), 6c the layouts and the stack tail-completion seam\n(needs 5a/5b).\n\n---\n\n## 1. The canonical frame\n\n### 1.1 Shapes, as the evaluator mints them\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4 \"Delivery\", §4.2; evaluator design §1.3-1.6;\n`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:61-84` for `act-payload`,\n`entry-builders`, `tail-builders`; `:229-234` for the reified payload\nand the park). Two closed state shapes and one closed delivery record:\n\n```clojure\nact  = {:segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :k K :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd r}}\ntail = {:segment seg :site n :k K :deliver {:deliver :return}}\nret  = {:type :return :segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :rd r}     ; one frame kind of K\n```\n\n- `:pc` of an `act` is the resume pc, already `pc+1` of the parking\n  instruction; `:site` of a `tail` is the tail call's own pc (evaluator\n  §1.5).\n- `W` of an `act` is `saved(pc, rd) = W↾(L(pc) − {rd})`\n  (`analysis/saved`, `analysis.cljc:92-97`), built with `select-keys`,\n  so a nil-valued live register is a present key.\n- `K` is a vector of `ret` frames, innermost last, or nil when empty\n  (`put-registers`, `semantic_register.cljc:47-52`); each frame's window\n  is `saved(frame.pc, frame.rd)` of its own body.\n- A **parked record** (`:park`, and FFI bookkeeping under a call id) is\n  an `act` payload plus `:type :parked-continuation :id`\n  (`engine/park-continuation`; evaluator §1.6); the FFI bookkeeping\n  record of an effectful **tail** call is a `tail` payload (evaluator\n  §1.6).\n- A **reified continuation** is `values/continuation owner (assoc act\n  :type :reified-continuation)` (`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:229-232`).\n- The wait entry the engine holds is the `act` or `tail` payload plus\n  the engine's fields (`:reason`, `:cursor-ref`, `:stream-id`, `:datom`,\n  `:call-id`, `:request-sent`, the link fields), which travel as the\n  **pending** (§2.4), never as frame slots.\n- The task's **free environment** is the `:env` a `create-vm` received\n  (`semantic_register.cljc:261`): every activation's `E` is that map\n  merged under the lexical chain's bindings (§4.4), and a de Bruijn\n  kernel holds the same map as its task-wide `:free-env`\n  (`debruijn/stack.cljc:82, 418, 683`; `debruijn/register.cljc:50`).\n\n### 1.2 The UCF frame, keyed form\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §4.2), restated with one naming decision (§10.1 F1):\n\n```clojure\n;; act state\n{:yin.k/state :act\n :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/pc n\n :yin.k/env   {:yin.k/bindings {sym V …} :yin.k/store-of M?}\n :yin.k/window {r V …}                      ; exactly saved(pc, rd); sorted by r on the wire\n :yin.k/deliver {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}\n :yin.k/k [{:yin.k/frame-type :return :yin.k/segment A :yin.k/pc n\n            :yin.k/env E :yin.k/window {r V …} :yin.k/rd r} …]\n :yin.k/pending {…}}                        ; absent on a parked record and a reified value\n\n;; tail state\n{:yin.k/state :tail\n :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/site n\n :yin.k/deliver {:yin.k/deliver :return}\n :yin.k/k [ … ]\n :yin.k/pending {…}}\n```\n\nThis keyed form is the **decoded canonical frame**: what the validator\nof §3 judges, what the layouts of §4 produce and consume, and what the\nharness compares across lanes. The rows of §2 are its carrier and the\ntwo are in bijection (§2.6).\n\n---\n\n## 2. The row carrier\n\nThe row-carrier draft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`) is adopted\n**except** where it describes the stack-shaped machine or legacy\nreaders; §10.3 lists every replaced or deleted clause. Its §3 (rows, id\nspaces, census, the explicit \"version 3 verifies under BLAKE3\npermanently\" rule), §4 (value union), §5.1 (value rows, except\n`:reified`), §5.3 (pending), §5.4 (task context, except\n`:parked-table`), §6 (canonical cursor-cell numbering), §7 (equality) and §8\nsteps 1-6 and 8-12 stand.\n\n### 2.1 Positioning: body version 3, one profile\n\n**DECIDED** by ruling 2 and ruling 6. The carrier is **body version 3**.\nVersions 0-2 are the old handoff's; the row reader refuses them at the\ngate (`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`) and never reads, normalises or upgrades\none. Their reader (`yin.vm.ucf.handoff`) is untouched by this phase and\nleaves with the stack-shaped machine at phase 8; that coexistence is\nsequencing, not permission for the row reader to accept a legacy body.\nThe wire unit is the draft's:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/handoff true  :yin.k/version 3  :yin.k/root T  :yin.k/rows [[id kind & slots] …]}\n```\n\n**PROPOSED** (reviewer: ACCEPTED). The profile map is\n`{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}` and nothing else; there is\n**no `:yin.k/engine` key**. The frame is the canonical machine's and\nengine-independent by ruling 6; the kernel that executes it is the\nreceiving composition's choice. The version-2 registry\n(`ucf/profiles`, `ucf.cljc:51-74`) is not consulted.\n\n### 2.2 Rows, ids, census\n\n**DECIDED** (draft §3, ruling 1). Every row is `[id kind & slots]`;\ncontent rows have `id = (jing/segment-key body)` under Jing's default\ndigest, BLAKE3 for version 3 permanently; identity rows are cursor cells\n`:yin.k/cc-<n>` numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`); the root carries\nthe cursor-cell census. (The words cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref and cell\nare used exactly as defined in UCF §7.5.3, \"Terminology: cursor, cursor\ncell, cursor-ref, cell\". Before 2026-10-12 the cursor cells were called\n\"cells\" and spelled `:yin.k/c-<n>`, `:cells`, `:cell-body`; the version-2\nhandoff carrier keeps those spellings.) One ordering rule: \"sorted\" is by the Jing canonical\nbytes of the key as spelled.\n\n### 2.3 The frame rows (replacing draft §5.2)\n\n**DECIDED** by frozen §4.2's last paragraph; **PROPOSED** names `:act`\nand `:tail` (§10.1 F1, reviewer: ACCEPTED):\n\n| Kind | Slots | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:act` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  k-cref  rd-N` | the delivery record is `{:deliver :rd :rd rd-N}` by kind |\n| `:tail` | `segment-A  site-N  k-cref` | delivery `{:deliver :return}` by kind; **no** env, window or pc slot |\n| `:window` | `r₁ V₁  r₂ V₂  …` sorted by `rᵢ` ascending, unique | `rᵢ` is `N`; **a nil-valued register is present as `rᵢ [:lit nil]`**; the empty window is `[:window]` |\n| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` outermost first | `[:k]` is the empty continuation |\n| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  rd-N` | one `:return` frame |\n| `:wait` | `state-cref  pending-cref` | `state-cref` is an `:act` or a `:tail` row |\n| `:reified` | `act-cref` | a captured continuation value; its act's `pc−1` is a `:current-continuation` |\n| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | unchanged |\n| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | unchanged (draft §5.1) |\n| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁  S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | unchanged |\n\n`:window`, `:bindings`, `:store` and every other tabled keyed row are\nstructural rows, never a `V`; the draft's \"a reference-free collection\nmust be `[:lit d]`\" rule is about guest collections. `rd` on `:act` is\nredundant with the instruction at `pc−1` and is checked (rule A2), as\n`pc` is redundant with the pending and checked.\n\n### 2.4 Pending rows\n\n**DECIDED** (draft §5.3, UCF §7.4.3, v2 amendment §2): one kind\n`:pending`; reasons `:next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request\n:link-response :install` with the draft's slots, unchanged. What\nchanges is only which state a pending rides on: a `:wait` pairs a\npending with an `:act` (write-result) **or a `:tail`** (through-return),\nthe frozen §4.1 table's two state columns. The 4b evaluator's in-VM\nmarkers (`:request-sent`, `:call-id`, `:op`, `:datom`,\n`:response-cursor`, `:response-stream`) are the pending's\n(`handoff.cljc:702-724`, `:3219-3229`).\n\n### 2.5 Captures, parked records, module contexts, the parked table\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4; evaluator §1.6; draft §5.1, §5.4).\n\n- A **reified continuation** value is `[:row B]`, `B` a `:reified\n  [act-cref]` row; the act has no pending, its `pc−1` is a\n  `:current-continuation`, `rd` is that instruction's destination, the\n  window is `saved(pc, rd)`.\n- The **parked table** is `[:parked-table P₁ state-cref₁ P₂ state-cref₂ …]`\n  sorted by `P`; a state is an `:act` whose `pc−1` is `:park` (an\n  explicit park), **or** FFI bookkeeping under a call id `P` that a live\n  `:ffi`/`:ffi-request` pending of the same task carries: an `:act` whose\n  `pc−1` is `:ffi-call`, or a `:tail` whose site is the parking tail\n  `:call`. An active `parked-id` never selects a bookkeeping record (v2\n  amendment §7.3, kept).\n- A **module context** is `E`'s `store-of`; the module store travels as\n  `[:module-store M store-cref]`; every `store-of` resolves to a carried\n  module store (draft §8 step 3).\n- **Install children** are whole `:task` rows in the child role (draft\n  §5.4, §5.5), unchanged.\n\n### 2.6 The root (replacing draft §5.5 in two slots)\n\n**PROPOSED**, with the reviewer's correction of the free environment:\n\n```clojure\n[T :task\n   version          ; 3\n   contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}\n   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n   role             ; :root | :child\n   id-counter       ; N\n   free-env-cref    ; NEW: a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (§6.3)\n   store-cref\n   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (§6.1)\n   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, in wait order     (:blocked, :parked)\n   parked-id        ; P or nil                                (:parked only)\n   result           ; V or nil                                (:halted only)\n   custody]         ; plain, draft §5.5's table\n```\n\nNo `layout` slot and no native image: those were the positional\nprofiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1). The custody table of the draft §5.5\nstands, with its version-0 comparison reworded (§10.3 R10).\n\n### 2.7 The bijection with the keyed frame\n\n**PROPOSED.** `rows->frame` and `frame->rows` (`yin.vm.ucf.rows`, §8.1)\nare inverse on valid row sets and canonical keyed frames: `:act` ↔\n`{:yin.k/state :act …}` with the deliver record synthesised from the\nkind and `rd`; `:tail` ↔ `{:yin.k/state :tail …}`; `:window` ↔\n`:yin.k/window` as a map (`[:lit nil]` ↔ a nil-valued key); `:k` ↔\n`:yin.k/k` in order. `rows->frame` reshapes and decides nothing the\nvalidator decides.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The frame validator\n\n### 3.1 The pure input contract\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §4.2: membership recomputed from A; representation,\nbody scope, site/pc pairing, destination equality, delivery mode,\nreturn-frame sites, `K` structure, pending compatibility). **PROPOSED**\n(reviewer: publish the complete contract):\n\n```clojure\n(frame/defect frame code context task)\n;; frame   : a decoded canonical frame (§1.2), any shape (the rules judge it)\n;; code    : {A vector}, every vector already code/well-formed? (step 4 of the carrier ran)\n;; context : :wait | :captured | :parked\n;; task    : decoded task context, plain data, no engine:\n;;           {:parked-key P-or-nil                 ; the parked-table key this frame sits under (:parked only)\n;;            :ffi-call-ids #{…}                   ; call ids of the task's live :ffi and :ffi-request pendings\n;;            :free-env {sym V …}}                 ; the root's free environment, decoded\n;; => nil, or {:yin.k/kind k :yin.k/path p …}\n\n(frame/task-defect task)\n;; the once-per-body rule X1 over the decoded task context (its :free-env);\n;; run before any frame of the body; => nil or {:yin.k/kind k :yin.k/path p …}\n```\n\nNamespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.frame`, file\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc`, requiring only\n`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `yin.vm.debruijn`\n(`resolve-name`, for the chain of rule E2) and `yin.vm`\n(`reserved-name?`). It knows no kernel and reads no engine: `task` is\ndecoded from the rows (the parked table's key, the pendings' call ids,\nthe root's `free-env`), never from a running machine. A frame a stack\nkernel lifted and one the register evaluator lifted are judged by the\nsame function on the same inputs.\n\n### 3.2 The rules\n\nRules run in the order listed; the first defect wins; every defect\ncarries `:yin.k/kind` and `:yin.k/path`. **Structural rules S1-S4 run\nbefore any dependent read**, so later rules may index freely. `a =\n(analysis/analyze v)` per segment; `body(p) = (code/body-index\n(code/body-ranges v) p)`; `k(body)` the body's register count\n(`code/parse`); `inst(p) = (nth v p)`; `chain(b)` the owner chain of\nbody `b` as phase-5 §1.2 (closure pcs innermost first) and `params(c) =\n(nth (nth v c) 2)`.\n\n**Structure (every frame).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| S1 | `frame` is a map; `:yin.k/state` is `:act` or `:tail`; an `act` has exactly the keys `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k` plus optionally `:yin.k/pending`; a `tail` exactly `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/site :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k` plus optionally `:yin.k/pending` | `:frame-shape` |\n| S2 | `:yin.k/k` is a vector; every element is a map with exactly `:yin.k/frame-type :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/rd`, `:yin.k/frame-type` is `:return` | `:k-shape` (path `[:yin.k/k i]`) |\n| S3 | every `:yin.k/window` present (the act's, each return frame's) is a map whose keys are exact non-negative integers (on the wire: sorted ascending, unique) and whose values are `V` | `:window-representation` |\n| S4 | every `:yin.k/env` present is `{:yin.k/bindings {S V …}}` optionally with `:yin.k/store-of M`; `:yin.k/deliver` is a map | `:env-shape` / `:deliver-shape` |\n\n**Code (every frame).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| C1 | `:yin.k/segment` of the frame and of every return frame names a key of `code` | `:segment` |\n| C2 | an `act`'s `pc`, every return frame's `pc` and a `tail`'s `site` are **exact non-negative integers** (`cbor/numeric-kind` `:integer` for a wire value; never a float carrier), checked **before** any indexing; then `0 < pc < (count v)` and `0 ≤ site < (count v)` | `:pc-kind` / `:pc-bounds` |\n| C3 | `body(pc−1) = body(pc)` for the act and every return frame (no body ends in a parking instruction, frozen §3.4 rule 6) | `:pc-body` |\n\n**The current activation (`act` only; a `tail` skips A1-A6).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| A1 | `inst(pc−1)` is one of `:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation`, or a `:call` with `tail?` false | `:site` |\n| A2 | `(:yin.k/rd deliver) = (code/rd inst(pc−1))` and `deliver = {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}` exactly | `:destination` / `:deliver` |\n| A3 | every window key `< k(body(pc))` | `:window-scope` |\n| A4 | `rd ∉ (keys window)` | `:window-self` |\n| A5 | `(set (keys window)) ⊆ (analysis/saved a pc rd)` | `:window-extra` |\n| A6 | `(analysis/saved a pc rd) ⊆ (set (keys window))` | `:window-missing` |\n\n**The tail site (`tail` only).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| T1 | `inst(site)` is a `:call` with `tail?` true; `deliver = {:yin.k/deliver :return}` exactly | `:site` / `:deliver` |\n\n**Return frames (every frame, each `K[i]`).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| K1 | `inst(frame.pc−1)` in `frame.segment` is a `:call` with `tail?` false | `:return-site` |\n| K2 | `frame.rd = (code/rd inst(frame.pc−1))` | `:return-destination` |\n| K3 | every key of `frame.window` `< k(body(frame.pc))`; `frame.rd ∉ keys`; `(set (keys frame.window)) = (analysis/saved aₛ frame.pc frame.rd)` in that segment's analysis, reported as `:return-window-scope` / `:return-window-self` / `:return-window-extra` / `:return-window-missing` | as named |\n\n**Environments (every `E`: the act's and each return frame's).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| E1 | no binding key is reserved (`vm/reserved-name?`) | `:reserved-name` |\n| E2 | **`E` is the free environment under the lexical chain.** With `C = (set (mapcat params (chain (body pc))))` the lexically bound names at that pc (a return frame's chain is that of its own `pc`): (a) **every name of `C` is a key of `bindings`**, a nil binding being a present key (`bind-params` nil-fills, so a lexical name is never absent; an absent one would be nil-filled on lower and break the canonical round trip); (b) `(apply dissoc bindings C) = (apply dissoc (:free-env task) C)` as maps of `V`: a key outside `C` the free environment lacks, a free key `E` lacks, or a differing value, is a defect | `:free-env-mismatch` |\n\n**Task (once per body, before any frame; `frame/task-defect`).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| X1 | the root's `free-env` decodes to a map `{S V …}` with symbol keys, sorted and unique on the wire, none reserved (`vm/reserved-name?`, the same check `create-vm` runs on its `:env`, `vm/check-bindings!`, `vm.cljc:161-168`); it is the `:free-env` every E2 compares against | `:free-env-shape` / `:reserved-name` (path `[:free-env]`) |\n\n**Pending and contexts.**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| P1 | `context :wait` requires `:yin.k/pending` present; `:captured` and `:parked` require it absent | `:pending-presence` |\n| P2 | (`:wait`) the pending's reason is admitted by `inst(pc−1)` (act) or `inst(site)` (tail): `:stream-next → :next`; `:stream-put → :put`; `:ffi-call → :ffi, :ffi-request`; `:call → :next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request :link-response :install`; `:park` and `:current-continuation` admit none (frozen §4.1; `wire-of-kind`, `handoff.cljc:491-504`; ucf-revisions §8) | `:reason-mismatch` |\n| P3 | (`:captured`) the frame is an `act` and `inst(pc−1)` is `:current-continuation` | `:captured-site` |\n| P4 | (`:parked`) either `inst(pc−1)` is `:park` (an `act`), or `(:parked-key task) ∈ (:ffi-call-ids task)` and the frame is an `act` with `inst(pc−1) = :ffi-call` or a `tail` whose `inst(site)` is a tail `:call` (bookkeeping, evaluator §1.6) | `:parked-site` |\n\nRule E2 is the correction the review required (§4.4): a merged `E`\ncannot be split into free and lexical parts without the task's free\nenvironment, so the carrier carries it and the validator checks that\nevery `E` agrees with it off the lexical names. Nothing here reads a\nstack depth, a stack base or a `live` vector.\n\n### 3.3 Where and when the validator runs\n\n**DECIDED** (carrier order kept: draft §8, step 7 after step 6; the\nreview's ruling). On **lower**: gate (1), structure (2), graph (3),\nhashes (4), canonical form (5), canonical numbering (6), **frames (7)**,\npending and operations (8), dependency closure (9), custody (10),\ncomposition (11), restore (12). In step 7 the body's decoded task\ncontext is judged first by `frame/task-defect` (X1), then every `:act`,\n`:tail`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and parked-table state is decoded and\njudged by `frame/defect` with its context and that task context; a defect\nis `:yin.k/undecodable` with the rule's kind and the row path. A\nfirst-defect test therefore sees a numbering defect\n(`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`) before a frame defect on a body that has both,\nand §8's acceptance items say so. On **lift**: the same function runs\nover every assembled frame before any row is emitted; a frame the\nevaluator itself minted that fails is `:yin.k/non-portable`\n`:inconsistent-state`. The harness runs it over every frame a layout\nreconstructs.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Layouts: reconstructing the canonical frame\n\n### 4.1 What a layout is\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §4.3; UCF §7.4.2). A foreign engine publishes a\nstatic map per segment and engine (`A`, resume pcs, `def`/`use`/`L`\nfrom `analysis/analyze`, `lexically-required`, and a layout from\nphysical location to virtual register) and reconstructs the canonical\nframe from its state at a safepoint; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` on\nadmitted canonical frames. Two kernels are intended to reconstruct\ncanonical frames: the de Bruijn register kernel over `R(A)` and the de\nBruijn stack kernel over `H(A)` (phase 5). The AST walker is not (frozen\n§10 phase 6 names the three kernels; the walker has no pc).\n\n**PROPOSED** namespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout`, file\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, requiring\n`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `.derive` (phase 5),\n`yin.vm.debruijn` (`resolve-name`), `yin.vm.engine` (`bind-params`), and\nthe two kernels' public state. Public: `register-layout`,\n`stack-layout` (static, per segment), `lift-act`, `lift-tail`,\n`lower-act`, `lower-tail` (per kernel, dispatching on the kernel's\n`link-format`).\n\n### 4.2 The register kernel's layout\n\n**DECIDED** by phase-5 §2.4 (R's bodies and pcs are A's) and §2.3\n(`R(v) = locals + slot(v)`); the kernel's payload shape from\n`debruijn/register.cljc:690-704` (call frame: `:site-pc :return-pc\n:frames :regs :live :dest :store-of`) and `write-back` (`:455-485`:\n`:write-result` rebuilds a dense register vector from `regs` and writes\n`dest`; `:return-result` runs the return transition).\n\n**Static half.** For segment `A` with `R = (:image (derive/register-image\nv))`: for every pc `p`, `v[p]` names virtual registers in its `rd`,\n`:reg` and `:regs` slots and `R[p]` names physical registers in the same\nslots. `phys(body, v)` is read off any pc that names `v`; it is total on\nthe virtuals a body names. Its inverse is **restricted**: `virt(p, s)`\nis defined only for `s` in the physical image of\n`live-out*(p) = ⋃ L(q) for q ∈ (analysis/successors v p)` (the actual\nsuccessors, phase-5 §2.5; empty for a tail call) at a **reachable**\nboundary pc `p` (`analysis/reachable`); there it is \"the `v ∈ live-out*(p)`\nwith `phys(v) = s`\", single-valued because the allocator's intervals\ncover every live point and distinct live virtuals hold distinct slots\n(a property the harness checks, §5.6 item 3, never assumed). Physical\noperands of **unreachable** layout-syntax instructions (the structural\nslot shared by several definition-less ids, phase-5 §2.2) are not\ninverted; they are validated separately by the kernel's own\n`register-image-defect` and by phase-5's gates, and never reach a\ncanonical frame (no reachable instruction reads them).\n\n**Lift, `act`.** From the kernel's wait entry with `:resume-mode\n:write-result` (payload `:image :site-pc :pc :frames :regs :live\n:continuation :dest :store-of`, v2 amendment §5.3):\n\n1. `A` = the A the image row of the entry's `:image` was derived from\n   (the kernel's code space is keyed by A after phase 5; R is its\n   checksum).\n2. relative `pc` = the entry's `pc` minus the image row's offset\n   (`image-row`/`absolute-pc`, `debruijn/register.cljc:326-337`); R's pcs\n   are A's, so this is the canonical pc.\n3. `rd = (code/rd (nth v (dec pc)))` **from A**, never the physical\n   `:dest` (which is `phys(rd)`).\n4. `W = {virt(pc−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ regs}`; the kernel's `live` is\n   `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(pc−1) rd)))` (the subtraction is on\n   **virtual** ids before mapping: on the worked example `live-out*(3) =\n   L(4) = {1 2}`, `(disj … 2) = {1}`, `phys → [0]`; subtracting the\n   physical `3` from `{1 2}` would wrongly keep `2`), so `(keys W) =\n   saved(pc, rd)`, which rule A5/A6 then checks.\n5. `E` = §4.4 from the entry's `frames` and `store-of`, at `body(pc)`.\n6. `K`: for each continuation frame `f`, **independently**: its\n   `return-pc` is absolute in the kernel's concatenated code space, so\n   first its image row `rowₖ = (image-row images return-pc)` and relative\n   `pcₖ = return-pc − offsetₖ`, then `Aₖ` of that row (frames may cross\n   images: a module closure called from another image returns into its\n   caller's image), `rdₖ = (code/rd (nth vₖ (dec pcₖ)))` from `Aₖ`, never\n   `f.dest`; `Wₖ = {virt_{Aₖ}(pcₖ−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ f.regs}`; `Eₖ` = §4.4\n   from `f.frames` and `f.store-of` at `body(pcₖ)` in `Aₖ`.\n7. pending: the engine fields of the entry, as `lift-pending!` maps them\n   (`handoff.cljc:674-749`).\n\n**Lift, `tail`.** From an entry with `:resume-mode :return-result`\n(`dest nil`, `live []`): `A` and the relative `site = site-pc` as in 1-2\n(the entry's `site-pc` is the tail call; its `pc` is `site+1`); `K` as\nin 6; pending as in 7; **no** `W`, `E` or `pc` are read: the entry's\n`regs` (empty by `live []`) and `frames` are discarded (frozen §2.4: a\ntail state carries no window and no `E`).\n\n**Lower, `act`.** Given a canonical `act` and the receiver's attached\nimage for `A` (derived by `derive/register-image` from the carried\nvector, offset `off`): `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + pc − 1`;\n`:pc = off + pc`; `:regs = (sort-by first [[phys(v) V] for [v V] in W])`;\n`:live = (sort (map phys (keys W)))` (the kernel's `live-exact` rule\nrecomputes `body-liveness` and must agree); `:dest = phys(rd)`;\n`:resume-mode :write-result`; `:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of`\nfrom `E`; `:continuation` = for each `K[i]`, with its own `Aₖ`'s image\nrow `offₖ`: `{:site-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ −1) :return-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ) :regs\n… :live … :dest (phys_{Aₖ} rdₖ) :frames (§4.4 from Eₖ) :store-of …}`;\n`:format reffects/format-tag`, `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's current\naggregate (never carried). Engine fields from the pending\n(`lower-frame!`, `handoff.cljc:3182-3246`), `:deliver` carried.\n\n**Lower, `tail`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + site`;\n`:pc = off + site + 1`; `:regs []`; `:live []`; `:dest nil`;\n`:resume-mode :return-result`; `:frames []` and `:store-of nil`\n(unused: `write-back`'s `:return-result` branch reads `:frames` and\n`:store-of` only to assign them before the return transition replaces\nthem from the popped frame, `register.cljc:461-468`; the harness checks\nthat the values chosen here are never observable, §5.6 item 8);\n`:continuation` as for `act`. The register kernel already supports this\ndelivery (`:return-result`, `register.cljc:458-468`).\n\n### 4.3 The stack kernel's layout\n\n**DECIDED** by phase-5 §3.2 (the emitter's walk) and the kernel's call\nand return arms (`debruijn/stack.cljc:716-776`): a non-tail `:call`\npops `f` and the `argc` operands, leaving `stack'`, and pushes\n`{:return-pc (inc pc) :frames frames :stack-base (count stack')}`\n(`:728-733`); `:return` truncates to the frame's `stack-base` and pushes\nthe value (`:766-772`); a host-function effect parks with the\ncontinuation after the call site and `stack-restore` resumes by conjing\nthe value at the entry's `pc` (`:748-752`, `:555-568`).\n\n**Static half.**\n\n1. **pc correspondence.** The stack emitter visits parse nodes in\n   `project`'s order and emits one instruction per A instruction plus a\n   `:push` after each child of a call, each `:stream-put` target and\n   each `:ffi-call` operand (phase-5 §3.2). So H **with every `:push`\n   removed** corresponds one-to-one, in order, with A: the k-th non-push\n   H instruction ↔ A pc `k`; `h->a`, `a->h` are the two directions. The\n   harness checks mnemonic families agree at every k (§5.6 item 4).\n2. **operand layout.** For an A pc `p` and the parse node at `p`,\n   `stack-layout(p)` is the vector, bottom to top, of the virtual\n   registers whose values are pushed and not yet consumed while control\n   is inside `p`'s expression: for each ancestor `n` and the child `c` on\n   the path to `p`, the children of `n` before `c` that `n` pushes (a\n   `:call`: the operator and the operands before `c`; a `:stream-put`:\n   the target when `c` is the value; an `:ffi-call`: the operands before\n   `c`; `:if`, `:define`, `:stream-cursor/next/close`, `:resume`: none).\n   **Checked** (§5.6 item 5): for a **non-tail** parking instruction,\n   `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p) (code/rd v[p]))`.\n   For a **tail** `:call` the canonical state has no window and\n   `stack-layout(p)` is not consulted (it may be non-empty for a tail\n   call in operand position, whose pushed siblings are dead layout\n   syntax).\n\n**K slices (corrected).** Let the kernel's continuation be\n`[f₀ … f_{m−1}]` outermost first with bases `b₀ … b_{m−1}` and the\noperand stack `St` of length `n`. Because `bᵢ = (count stack')` is the\ncaller's residual height **after** popping the callee's operands, the\nslice of operands belonging to activation `i` is\n\n```\nslice(f₀)      = St[0, b₀)\nslice(fᵢ)      = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)        for 0 < i < m\nslice(current) = St[b_{m−1}, n)         ; or St[0, n) when K is empty\n```\n\n(the review's correction: `[previous-base, base_i)` with previous-base\n0, and the current activation `[last-base, count)`).\n\n**Lift, `act`.** From a wait entry `{:image :pc :frames :stack\n:continuation :store-of}` (v2 amendment §5.2) whose `pc−1` in H is **not**\na tail `:call`:\n\n1. `A` from the image row; relative H pc `pcₕ = pc − off`; canonical\n   `pc = h->a(pcₕ − 1) + 1`; `rd = (code/rd v[pc−1])`.\n2. `W = (zipmap (stack-layout (dec pc)) slice(current))`; the two have\n   equal length (checked) and `(keys W) = saved(pc, rd)` (rule A5/A6).\n3. `E` = §4.4 from `frames` and `store-of` at `body(pc)`.\n4. `K`: for `fᵢ` **independently**: its `return-pc` is absolute; row and\n   relative `pcₕᵢ` through the stack kernel's own offset table\n   (`debruijn/stack.cljc:355`, `absolute-pc`'s inverse); `Aᵢ` of that row;\n   `pcᵢ = h->a_{Aᵢ}(pcₕᵢ − 1) + 1`; `rdᵢ = (code/rd vᵢ[pcᵢ−1])`;\n   `Wᵢ = (zipmap (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) slice(fᵢ))`; `Eᵢ` = §4.4\n   from `fᵢ.frames` and `fᵢ.store-of`.\n5. pending from the engine fields.\n\n**Lift, `tail`.** From a wait entry whose `pc−1` in H is a tail `:call`:\n`A` and `site = h->a(pcₕ − 1)`; `K` as in 4; pending; the entry's\n`frames`, `store-of` and `slice(current)` are **discarded** (dead\nlayout syntax; frozen §2.4).\n\n**Lower, `act`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:pc = off + a->h(pc−1) + 1`;\n`:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of` from `E`; `:stack` = the\nconcatenation over `K` outermost first of each `slice(fᵢ)` =\n`(mapv Wᵢ (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)))`, then `slice(current) =\n(mapv W (stack-layout (dec pc)))`; `:continuation` = for each `fᵢ`\n`{:return-pc (+ offᵢ (a->h_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) 1) :frames (§4.4 from Eᵢ)\n:stack-base bᵢ :store-of …}` where **`bᵢ` is the cumulative length of\nthe slices up to and including `slice(fᵢ)`**: `b₀ = |slice(f₀)|`, `bᵢ =\nb_{i−1} + |slice(fᵢ)|`. This is the inverse of the lift's\n`slice(fᵢ) = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)`: the kernel's base is the caller's\nresidual height, which is exactly the end of the caller's own slice\n(`stack.cljc:731-733`). On the multi-frame fixture of §8.3 item 6\n(five return frames, slices `[0,0) [0,0) [0,0) [0,2) [2,4)`) this emits\nbases `0, 0, 0, 2, 4`; the before-slice rule would emit `0, 0, 0, 0, 2`\nand the kernel would truncate to the wrong height on return. `:format\nstack/format-tag`; `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's. `stack-restore`\nthen conjes the delivered value onto `:stack` at `:pc`, the `:push`-free\nposition after the parking instruction, exactly where H expects the\nresult.\n\n**Lower, `tail`, and the tail-completion seam.** Today the stack kernel\nresumes a parked host effect at the entry's `pc` by pushing the value\n(`stack-restore`, `:566`); for a **tail** call that resumes the body at\n`a->h(site)+1`, which then runs the layout syntax after the tail call\n(for a tail call in operand position: the remaining pushes and the\nenclosing call) **before** reaching `:return`. That is not the canonical\nthrough-return delivery (frozen §2.4, §4.1 \"effectful call, tail\":\ndelivery **through return** against `K`). The layout therefore cannot\nlower a canonical `tail` into today's stack kernel. **PROPOSED, gated\n(§8.3 slice 6c-pre):** the stack kernel gains a tail-completion seam:\nits park-entry builders mark an entry whose parking instruction is a\ntail `:call` with `:resume-mode :return-result` (the register kernel's\nexisting vocabulary, `register.cljc:458-468`), and `stack-restore`\ndelivers such an entry by the **return transition** (pop the top frame,\ntruncate `:stack` to its base, push the value, restore its `frames`,\n`store-of` and `return-pc`; halt with the value when `K` is empty) instead\nof resuming at `pc`; the retained-FFI re-park and the response decoding\n(`stack-restore`'s `:request-sent` and `:call-id` steps, `:548-557`) keep\ntheir order before the final delivery, as in the register kernel.\nLowering a canonical `tail` then produces\n`{:image row-of-A :pc (+ off (a->h site) 1) :resume-mode :return-result\n:stack (concat of the K slices, in order) :frames [] :store-of nil\n:continuation …}` with each continuation frame's `:stack-base bᵢ` the\n**same cumulative-including rule as the `act` lowering** (the last\nframe's base equals the stack's length, since the current activation\ncontributes no slice); the `:frames`/`:store-of` values are unobservable\n(the return transition replaces them) and the harness checks that (§5.6\nitem 8). File:\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc` (not a 4b or phase-5 file); the\nregister kernel needs no change.\n\n### 4.4 Positional lexical frames, the free environment and `E`\n\n**DECIDED** by frozen §2.1 and §2.4 (`E_c[ps ↦ a]` by `bind-params`),\nphase-5 §1.2 (the owner chain), and the kernels' resolution of free\nnames through the task-wide `free-env` (`stack.cljc:683`:\n`(engine/resolve-var free-env …)`). Both de Bruijn kernels hold the\nlexical environment as positional frames `F = [[V …] …]` outermost first,\none per enclosing closure activation, plus the task-wide `free-env`.\n\n**Forward (F → E), lossless.** At a pc of body `b` with\n`chain = [c₀ … c_{j}]` outermost first (phase-5's `chain` reversed):\n\n```\nE = (merge free-env\n           (bind-params params(c₀) F[0])\n           …\n           (bind-params params(c_j) F[j]))\n    ∪ {engine/store-of-key M}  when the activation's closure carries store-of M\n```\n\nThis reproduces the register evaluator's `E` on closure entry\n(`semantic_register.cljc:141-143`: `(merge (:env c) (bind-params\n(:params c) args))`, `(:env c)` being the creating activation's `E`,\nitself `free-env` merged with the outer chain), including rightmost-wins\nduplicates, nil-fill and extra-argument dropping, which are\n`engine/bind-params`'s.\n\n**Inverse (E → F), and why the free environment travels.** The reviewer's\ncounterexample, with initial free `x = 99`:\n\n```clojure\n(let [f (fn [] x)]\n  ((fn [x] (f (stream-next c))) 1))\n```\n\nAt the blocked read the current `E` binds `x` to 1 (the parameter). The\nearlier revision recovered the receiver's free environment as \"`E` minus\nthe chain's names\", which drops `x` entirely, and the lowered de Bruijn\nclosure `f`, whose body is `[:load-free x]`, resolves `x` through the\ntask-wide `free-env` and loses 99. **So the task's free environment is\ncarried explicitly** (root slot `free-env`, §2.6; §6.3), every `E` is\nchecked against it off the lexical names (rule E2), and the inverse is:\n\n```\nfree-env  := the root's free-env row (never derived from any E)\nF[i]      := (mapv #(get E %) params(cᵢ))      for i = 0 … j\n```\n\n`F[i]` for an **outer** frame whose parameter is shadowed by an inner\nframe receives the inner value, not the outer one; that value is\nunobservable from `body(pc)` (resolution picks the innermost binder,\n`resolve-name`, `debruijn.cljc:617-637`), and the canonical frame does\nnot carry it (frozen §2.1: parameters live in `E`, and `E` holds one\nvalue per name). The round-trip law is therefore stated on **canonical\nframes** (`lift(lower(frame)) = frame`), not on kernel state, and the\nharness's `E` check (§5.6 item 6) compares the layout's `E` with the\nevaluator's; shadowed outer positional values are not a claim of this\ndesign. The free bindings that *are* observable, by any closure's\n`:load-free`, are preserved exactly by the root slot.\n\n**Closure values.** A de Bruijn closure (positional marker: segment,\nentry, arity, frames `F`, store-of) reconstructs to the canonical\nclosure (`{:type :closure :params :entry :segment :env}`, evaluator\n§1.8): `params` from A's `:closure` tuple at the owner pc of the entry\nbody, `env` by the forward rule over the closure's captured `F` and the\ntask's `free-env`, `entry` the body pc. The inverse reads `F` back from\n`env` by the chain. Checked both ways (§5.6 item 7).\n\n### 4.5 What a layout never does\n\n**DECIDED.** A layout reads a kernel's state, A and the carried task\ncontext; it never runs code, never fills a binding from the receiver's\nstore or free environment (the free environment is the **carried** one,\n§6.3), never reads a native image's hash as an identity, and never\ninvents a safepoint: a kernel pc whose predecessor is not a parking\ninstruction is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2).\n\n---\n\n## 5. The safepoint harness\n\n### 5.1 What it proves\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §10 phase 6 gate; UCF §7.11). For every row of the\nexpanded matrix, both delivery modes, nested and reified captures,\nmodule contexts and every refusal: lift at the safepoint, lower into a\nfresh machine, continue, and result and effect trace equal the\nuninterrupted reference run; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame`; the frame's\nreason is the observed wait's; on the three lanes: `:semantic-register`,\n`:stack-through-layout`, `:register-through-layout`.\n\n### 5.2 The matrix\n\n**Expected results are stated for the bare expression.** Each row runs\nunder the four segment wrappers of the old harness\n(`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:92-106`), whose expected results follow\nmechanically from the bare result `r`: `:bare` → `r`; `:operand` →\n`[:operand r]` (`(cat [:operand] r)`); `:depth-3` → `[3 [2 [1 r]]]`\n(`nest` wraps `(cat [d] ((fn [x] …) d))` three times, as the old test's\npinned `[2 [1 [:two 2 \"C\"]]]` shows for depth 2, `:574`); `:captured` →\n`[:captured :arg r]` (`(cat (cat (cat [] k) y) r)` with `k = :captured`,\n`y = :arg`). **Frames the wrappers add.** The harness marks `:tail?\ntrue` only where a row says so (`:228`, `:256`; `ast->semantic-bytecode`\ndefaults it to false, `vm.cljc:1089`; `mark-tail` is the expander's and\nis not run here), so every `let1` and every `nest` application is a\nnon-tail call that pushes a return frame. `with-cat` (`:69-76`,\noutermost in every segment, `:96`) adds **one** frame below everything\nthe row builds, with `stack-base 0` and an empty slice on the stack lane\n(nothing is pushed before its application). On top of it `:bare` and\n`:operand` add none of their own (the row is the operand), `:depth-3`\nadds three (`nest`'s applications, each with two values, `cat` and\n`[d]`, pushed under it), and `:captured` adds three (`let1 k`, `let1 f`,\n`(f :arg)`). A row's own `let1`s add theirs. Row 20 alone runs outside\nthe wrappers.\n\nPrograms use `v2_support`'s AST helpers (`v2_support.cljc:56-104`:\n`lit v lam app def! then cursor-of next-of let1`) and the four segments\nof the old harness (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:92-106`: `:bare`,\n`:operand`, `:depth-3`, `:captured`); `cat` is the closure `(fn [p q]\n(conj p q))` bound around every body (`:69-81`). \"Expected\" is the\nreference run's normalised result; where a row says \"executable\" it runs\non all three lanes; \"synthetic\" means the frame is built by hand and\njudged by `frame/defect` only, because no program reaches it on the\nlanded or stage-2 evaluator.\n\n| # | Row | Program (inside each segment) | How parked / satisfied | State, pending | Expected after satisfy | Executable? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | blocked read | `(let1 s (stream/make 4) (let1 c (cursor-of s) (let1 a (next-of c) (cat (cat [] a) (next-of c)))))`, `\"A\"` appended before the run | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next` | `[\"A\" \"B\"]` | yes |\n| 2 | blocked write | `(stream-put w \"v\")` on a one-slot stream warmed with `:warmed` | take one | act, `:put` with retained `\"v\"` | `\"v\"` landed | yes |\n| 3 | FFI sent | `(dao.stream.apply/call :op/echo \"hello\")` | append the success response | act, `:ffi` | `\"echo!\"` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 4 | FFI retained | as 3 with a one-slot, warmed call-in | free the slot, then answer | act, `:ffi-request` | `\"echo!\"` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 5 | effectful call, non-tail, stream | `(cat [:ordinary] (stream/next! c))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next` | `[:ordinary \"B\"]` | yes (stage 1 has streams; `stream/next!` is the stream module's) |\n| 6 | **effectful call, tail, stream** | `((fn [u] (stream/next! c)) 0)` with the inner call `:tail? true` | append `\"B\"` | **tail**, `:next` | `\"B\"` delivered through `K` | yes |\n| 7 | link request phase | `(require 'host.mod)` parked **before** the request append succeeds (one-slot, warmed request stream) | free the slot, then answer `:ok` | act, `:link-request` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 8 | link response phase | `(require 'host.mod)` polling (request appended) | append the `:ok` response | act, `:link-response` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 9 | **link, tail** | `((fn [u] (require 'host.mod)) 0)` tail | as 8 | **tail**, `:link-response` | `'host.mod` through `K` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 10 | install, child running | `(require 'host.mod)` where `host.mod`'s body reads a stream the test has not fed, so the child is `:blocked` and the parent's wait is `:install` | feed the child's stream | act, `:install` + install entry, child kind `:blocked`, phase `:running` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 11 | install, child parked | as 10 with the child body executing `:park` | resume the child by id | act, `:install`, child kind `:parked`, phase `:parked` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 12 | install, tail | `((fn [u] (require 'host.mod)) 0)` tail with 10's child | as 10 | **tail**, `:install` | `'host.mod` through `K` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 13 | explicit park | `(vm/park)` | `(vm/resume pid \"back\")` beside | parked record, root `:parked` | `\"back\"` | yes |\n| 14 | reified capture live in a window | `(let1 k (vm/current-continuation) (cat (cat [] k) (next-of c)))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `k` is a `:reified` value in `W` | `[:continuation \"B\"]` normalised | yes |\n| 15 | capture invoked after lower | `(let1 k (vm/current-continuation) (if (= k :second) :done (let1 r (next-of c) (k :second))))`: on the first pass `k` is a continuation, so the test fails and the read runs and parks; after satisfaction `(k :second)` delivers `:second` into the capture's `rd` (the `let1` operand) and re-enters at the capture's successor pc, so the body is entered again with `k = :second` and the test succeeds **without repeating the read** (the invocation is abortive on every lane: `:continuation` operator kind, `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:212-217` stage 2, `stack.cljc:760-763`). `if` is `{:type :if :test :consequent :alternate}` (`vm.cljc:1069`), a helper the new harness file adds; `=` is the primitive (`vm.cljc:387`), consumed before the read | append `\"x\"` after lower | act, `:next`; `k` is a `:reified` value in `E` (the closure's) | `:done` | yes |\n| 16 | nested capture | `(let1 k1 (vm/current-continuation) (let1 k2 (vm/current-continuation) (cat (cat (cat [] k1) k2) (next-of c))))` | append `\"B\"` | act; `k2`'s act window holds `k1` (a `:reified` inside a `:reified`'s window) | `[:continuation :continuation \"B\"]` | yes |\n| 17 | module context, blocked inside a module closure | `host.mod` exports `f = (fn [c] (stream/next! c))`; root: `(f c)` after `(require 'host.mod)` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`, `E` carries `store-of host.mod` | `\"B\"` | 4b stage 2 (link) |\n| 18 | module-store snapshot | `parked-module-store-cursors` (`lift_support.cljc:310-333`): an explicit park whose module store holds two cursors named by a module closure | resume | parked record; `:module-store` row | the two cursor cells | yes |","numLines":847,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1762,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012HTWB35TEHB3Qj1qQggXGQ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","